        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The families Trygonidae and Myliobatidae have carried the rajiform undulations a step further. The tail has become a mere trailing wisp, which, while it may have some slight course-determining action, appears to trail passively after the animal. Steering is accomplished for the most part by differential action of the pectorals. That is, in curving, the outside “wing” is speeded up or the inside one retarded which swings the animal accordingly.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


