        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/gait"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-07-07T16:49:55.659242Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Swimming-gait in which convergent rays in a convex-angled fin propogate a wave along the terminal edge of its membrane, by moving the rays serially from side to side.

By varying the way different rays bend within the wave, a fin&apos;s longitudinal thrust can be directed.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>caudal undulation</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">vectoring-undulation gait</rdfs:label>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...the ability of fish to actively control tail shape... and hence modulation and vectoring of
force.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Caudal Undulation:... the rays are convergent instead of parallel.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The fin-rays also perform small longitudinal as well as lateral movements, and they tend to be held like an open fan.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>Two forms may be observed: the first involving a rippling wave, the second a reversible standing wave. Breder says:

&quot;The central ray of the tail remains still and simply twists on its axis in the latter, while in the former all rays pass through a complete cycle of oscillation. In the method described by Du Bois-Reymond, the upper part of the tail is given a backward thrust diagonally to one side, while the other simultaneously is given a similar one to the opposite side. This has been observed well in Roccus , Epinephelus, and a few others, but appears to be much more rare than the previous method.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000034"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Swimming-gait in which convergent rays in a convex-angled fin propogate a wave along the terminal edge of its membrane, by moving the rays serially from side to side.

By varying the way different rays bend within the wave, a fin&apos;s longitudinal thrust can be directed.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000034"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>caudal undulation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
