        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Caudal Undulation:... the rays are convergent instead of parallel.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000034"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The fin-rays also perform small longitudinal as well as lateral movements, and they tend to be held like an open fan.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1109/48.757275</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000034"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Two forms may be observed: the first involving a rippling wave, the second a reversible standing wave. Breder says:

&quot;The central ray of the tail remains still and simply twists on its axis in the latter, while in the former all rays pass through a complete cycle of oscillation. In the method described by Du Bois-Reymond, the upper part of the tail is given a backward thrust diagonally to one side, while the other simultaneously is given a similar one to the opposite side. This has been observed well in Roccus , Epinephelus, and a few others, but appears to be much more rare than the previous method.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000035 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000035">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000682"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
