    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000036 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000036">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000411"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000048"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000662"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/group_behaviour"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-04-15T15:12:22.036312Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Aggregation that is 3-dimensional, characterized by slow individual motion and a relatively dense, disordered structure.

Swarm behavior allows the group to remain in an area.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">swarm</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>school</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>swarm aggregation</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Although the shape of copepod swarms depended on the species and the localities, the most common type was irregular ball-shaped. Each ball-shaped swarm was most often spherical or ovoid, but sometimes became stretched out longitudinally. These swarms were fairly distinct with clear boundaries. Their diameters ranged from a few centimeters to over l m, but most swarms were smaller than 50 cm in diameter. They usually stayed at the same position and contacted the bottom or were a little above it. Copepods within a swarm were hopping up and sinking down against the slight water current with their heads diagonally downward.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;By swarming of the young we mean swimming in very dense concentrations near some object.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000036"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Aggregation that is 3-dimensional, characterized by slow individual motion and a relatively dense, disordered structure.

Swarm behavior allows the group to remain in an area.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1371/journal.pcbi.1002915</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Swarms are characterized by slow individual motion and a relatively dense, disordered structure.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000036"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">swarm</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000036"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>school</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2023</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/28072abf0dcabdc270c0235a6fb16211/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
        <rdfs:comment>Whilst krill can no doubt &apos;school&apos; at times, as well as &apos;milling&apos; as explored in flumes in this paper, most literature regards &apos;swarming&apos; as non-polarized activity. Indeed this paper comments elsewhere on, &quot;krill transitioning from swarming to schooling&quot;.</rdfs:comment>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;In nature, they are mostly found as swarms in a polarized manner known as schools.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000036"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>swarm aggregation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000036"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Although the shape of copepod swarms depended on the species and the localities, the most common type was irregular ball-shaped. Each ball-shaped swarm was most often spherical or ovoid, but sometimes became stretched out longitudinally. These swarms were fairly distinct with clear boundaries. Their diameters ranged from a few centimeters to over l m, but most swarms were smaller than 50 cm in diameter. They usually stayed at the same position and contacted the bottom or were a little above it. Copepods within a swarm were hopping up and sinking down against the slight water current with their heads diagonally downward.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3354/meps011165</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000036"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;By swarming of the young we mean swimming in very dense concentrations near some object.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000037 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000037">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000034"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_4000164"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-07-07T20:03:40.754143Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Vectoring-undulation gait that involves the tail fin.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>CFP</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>caudal fin-wave propagation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>caudal undulation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">CFP gait</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;The propagation of a CFP is observable in a parrot cichlid that swims in still water or against a current. When a parrot cichlid initiates a forward sinking, the pectoral fins first induce a headdown pitching moment that tips the head down. Afterwards, the parrot cichlid adopts a stable tilted-down swimming posture. In this posture, the transverse amplitude (i.e. lateral displacement) of the dorsally propagating fin wave gradually increases from the ventral to the dorsal edges of the caudal fin.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000037"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Vectoring-undulation gait that involves the tail fin.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.5962/p.203769</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;While the caudal is operated primarily by the action of the body muscles, many fishes at times may be seen to undulate the tail fin vertically and move forward slowly without any other apparent effort.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000037"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>CFP</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2008</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.020263</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000037"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>caudal fin-wave propagation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2008</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.020263</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000037"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>caudal undulation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000037"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">CFP gait</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000037"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The propagation of a CFP is observable in a parrot cichlid that swims in still water or against a current. When a parrot cichlid initiates a forward sinking, the pectoral fins first induce a headdown pitching moment that tips the head down. Afterwards, the parrot cichlid adopts a stable tilted-down swimming posture. In this posture, the transverse amplitude (i.e. lateral displacement) of the dorsally propagating fin wave gradually increases from the ventral to the dorsal edges of the caudal fin.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.020263</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000038 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000038">
