    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000011"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-09-10T09:52:40.187376Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>living state</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>phase</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Component pattern in which animals express a characteristic set of basic building blocks over a long-lasting, stable time period.

Switches to new states are regulated by integration of the transitioning or prolonging influences of internal and external cues, as well as historical associations.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>bout</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">state</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>attitude</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>state pattern</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Among the most familiar of these behavioral states are easily observable arousal states like sleep and wakefulness, as well as feeding states controlled by hunger and satiety. Animals also exhibit emotional states, like states of heightened anxiety or depression, as well as cognitive states, for example, in the contexts of attention or working memory.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;In very cold water when Chaenobryttus is in a state of semi-hibernation they may sometimes be observed with no fin movement at all and with both sets of paired fins securely pressed against the sides. At such times the respiration is extremely superficial and appears to have little reactive effect. However, after a long period of time it was observed that they do move forward ever so slightly and that very occasionally the pectorals are brought into play to regain the previous position. However, they generally allow themselves to come in contact with some piece of brush or other object in which case the friction is sufficient to stop motion.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Behaviors may be regarded either as events or as states. Events are instantaneous; states have appreciable durations. Of course, in reality, the performance of any kind of behavior takes some amount of time, however brief. But if we consider behaviors at the moment of their onset, or at any other single defining instant, then we are recording events. We can, for example, record that an animal assumes a sitting posture, an act that occurs at an instant (and is therefore an event), or that the animal is seated (a state).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasBroadSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>living state</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasBroadSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>phase</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.27.3.264</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Component pattern in which animals express a characteristic set of basic building blocks over a long-lasting, stable time period.

Switches to new states are regulated by integration of the transitioning or prolonging influences of internal and external cues, as well as historical associations.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1534/genetics.120.303539</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;In each locomotion state, C. elegans animals express a characteristic set of locomotor parameters over a long-lasting, stable time period. C. elegans locomotion is comprised of just a few basic building blocks...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>bout</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2016</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/1365-2656.12379</dc:source>
        <rdfs:comment>&quot;...2 week bouts... 
...bouts of long distance movement...&quot;</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment>Given the relative nature of modules, sequences and states, bout is at times used correctly as a synonym for any of them.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">state</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>attitude</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>state pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Among the most familiar of these behavioral states are easily observable arousal states like sleep and wakefulness, as well as feeding states controlled by hunger and satiety. Animals also exhibit emotional states, like states of heightened anxiety or depression, as well as cognitive states, for example, in the contexts of attention or working memory.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1534/genetics.120.30353</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In very cold water when Chaenobryttus is in a state of semi-hibernation they may sometimes be observed with no fin movement at all and with both sets of paired fins securely pressed against the sides. At such times the respiration is extremely superficial and appears to have little reactive effect. However, after a long period of time it was observed that they do move forward ever so slightly and that very occasionally the pectorals are brought into play to regain the previous position. However, they generally allow themselves to come in contact with some piece of brush or other object in which case the friction is sufficient to stop motion.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Behaviors may be regarded either as events or as states. Events are instantaneous; states have appreciable durations. Of course, in reality, the performance of any kind of behavior takes some amount of time, however brief. But if we consider behaviors at the moment of their onset, or at any other single defining instant, then we are recording events. We can, for example, record that an animal assumes a sitting posture, an act that occurs at an instant (and is therefore an event), or that the animal is seated (a state).&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/4533591</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000002"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-09-10T15:53:05.898277Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Behaviour that describes the movement dynamics conditional on a behavioural state.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>act</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">action</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>active behavior</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>activity</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>activity behaviour</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...the bright red system in Hemichromis and the black in Tilapia natalensis are only seen when the fish also shows definite courtship activities...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:narrowMatch>NBO:0000338</skos:narrowMatch>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Behaviour that describes the movement dynamics conditional on a behavioural state.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1111/j.1600-0706.2011.19044.x</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Since animals change their movement patterns through time as a response to changing environmental factors, prey abundance, habitats etc., it is necessary to regard the system as a hierarchy of two subprocesses: An underlying behavior process that controls the switching between a number of diferent movement states; and a derived process that describes the movement dynamics conditional on the behavioral state.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>act</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">action</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>active behavior</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2002</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>activity</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>activity behaviour</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...the bright red system in Hemichromis and the black in Tilapia natalensis are only seen when the fish also shows definite courtship activities...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000011"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-09-10T19:12:57.574625Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Component pattern that involves sequential, nonrandom, transitions between behavioral elements.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>bout</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">chain</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>compound</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>sequence</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>chain pattern</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Head-shakes are very common and may occur in a variety of situations. They are characteristic of bouts of nibbling preening and are closely associated with bill-dipping. The usual sequence is: bill-dip + head-shake + preening movement.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;One example is dyadic fighting in male zebrafish, which can be divided into assessment and postresolution phases. Each phase is structured clearly in different behaviors. In the assessment phase, fish exhibit display, circle, and bite behaviors. In the resolution phase, agonistic behaviors (bite, chase, strike) are always initiated by the winner.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Spawning behaviors... in the nest-building minnows, the bluehead chub, Nocomis leptocephalus, and river chub, N. micropogon... were classified into six sequential categories (interim, approach, alignment, run, clasp, dissociation)...&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Component pattern that involves sequential, nonrandom, transitions between behavioral elements.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1089/zeb.2011.0690</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;This behavior sequence matrix was analyzed using a first-order Markov chain analysis to identify nonrandom transitions between behavioral elements (i.e., nonrandom temporal associations between behavioral patterns).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>bout</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
        <rdfs:comment>Given the relative nature of modules, sequences and states, bout is at times used correctly as a synonym for any of them.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">chain</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1966</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/4083155</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>compound</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>sequence</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</dc:date>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>chain pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Head-shakes are very common and may occur in a variety of situations. They are characteristic of bouts of nibbling preening and are closely associated with bill-dipping. The usual sequence is: bill-dip + head-shake + preening movement.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;One example is dyadic fighting in male zebrafish, which can be divided into assessment and postresolution phases. Each phase is structured clearly in different behaviors. In the assessment phase, fish exhibit display, circle, and bite behaviors. In the resolution phase, agonistic behaviors (bite, chase, strike) are always initiated by the winner.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1146/annurev-neuro-071714-033857</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000079"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Spawning behaviors... in the nest-building minnows, the bluehead chub, Nocomis leptocephalus, and river chub, N. micropogon... were classified into six sequential categories (interim, approach, alignment, run, clasp, dissociation)...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000011"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-09-10T19:19:45.70162Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Component pattern that involves a simple action, comprised of at least one body part movement, and produced from within a constrained set of behavioural possibilities.

The duration may be more or less extended.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>atomic element</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>bout</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>building block</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>element</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>module</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>motor primitive</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>moveme</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>unit</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>component</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>event</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>synergy</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>module pattern</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Hierarchical clustering separated bouts into seven types, corresponding to J-turns, approach swims, ‘‘slow 1’’ swims, capture strikes, ‘‘slow 2’’ swims, high-angle turns (HATs), and routine turns... J-turns, approach swims, and capture strikes occupy the prey capture region of the behavioral space... ‘‘Slow 2’’ swims, HATs, and routine turns were performed by fish during spontaneous swimming. ‘‘Slow 1’’ swims contributed to both prey capture and spontaneous behavior. Thus, taking transition dynamics into account, we could relate regions of our behavioral space to known bout types of zebrafish larvae and demonstrate the differential contributions of these swims to spontaneous and prey capture behavior.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Behaviors may be regarded either as events or as states. Events are instantaneous; states have appreciable durations. Of course, in reality, the performance of any kind of behavior takes some amount of time, however brief. But if we consider behaviors at the moment of their onset, or at any other single defining instant, then we are recording events. We can, for example, record that an animal assumes a sitting posture, an act that occurs at an instant (and is therefore an event), or that the animal is seated (a state).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;It is believed that complex, flexible behavior arises as a result of animals chaining together simpler, more stereotyped movements. These movements have been termed motor primitives, synergies, movemes, or behavioral modules. However, whether such modules truly constitute stereotyped, invariant movements or whether they merely reflect extremes in a behavioral continuum remains unclear.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>Behaviour modules may be stereotyped or lie on a continuum, partly depending on whether they rely on internal or external stimuli. An observor of a particular instance may of course label it as a &apos;module&apos; and add the characteristic &apos;flexible&apos; or &apos;inflexible&apos; should they wish, but for now this class will not be subdivided, nor one interpretation enforced, since knowing the origin of a stimulus is so difficult.</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Component pattern that involves a simple action, comprised of at least one body part movement, and produced from within a constrained set of behavioural possibilities.

The duration may be more or less extended.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>atomic element</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2025</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10988-025-09438-8</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>bout</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2019</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
        <rdfs:comment>Given the relative nature of modules, sequences and states, bout is at times used correctly as a synonym for any of them.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>building block</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2020</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1534/genetics.120.303539</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>element</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>module</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2019</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>motor primitive</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2019</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>moveme</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2019</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>unit</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</dc:date>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>component</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>event</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1974</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/4533591</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>synergy</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2019</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>module pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:date>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Hierarchical clustering separated bouts into seven types, corresponding to J-turns, approach swims, ‘‘slow 1’’ swims, capture strikes, ‘‘slow 2’’ swims, high-angle turns (HATs), and routine turns... J-turns, approach swims, and capture strikes occupy the prey capture region of the behavioral space... ‘‘Slow 2’’ swims, HATs, and routine turns were performed by fish during spontaneous swimming. ‘‘Slow 1’’ swims contributed to both prey capture and spontaneous behavior. Thus, taking transition dynamics into account, we could relate regions of our behavioral space to known bout types of zebrafish larvae and demonstrate the differential contributions of these swims to spontaneous and prey capture behavior.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Behaviors may be regarded either as events or as states. Events are instantaneous; states have appreciable durations. Of course, in reality, the performance of any kind of behavior takes some amount of time, however brief. But if we consider behaviors at the moment of their onset, or at any other single defining instant, then we are recording events. We can, for example, record that an animal assumes a sitting posture, an act that occurs at an instant (and is therefore an event), or that the animal is seated (a state).&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/4533591</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;It is believed that complex, flexible behavior arises as a result of animals chaining together simpler, more stereotyped movements. These movements have been termed motor primitives, synergies, movemes, or behavioral modules. However, whether such modules truly constitute stereotyped, invariant movements or whether they merely reflect extremes in a behavioral continuum remains unclear.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000080"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Behaviour modules may be stereotyped or lie on a continuum, partly depending on whether they rely on internal or external stimuli. An observor of a particular instance may of course label it as a &apos;module&apos; and add the characteristic &apos;flexible&apos; or &apos;inflexible&apos; should they wish, but for now this class will not be subdivided, nor one interpretation enforced, since knowing the origin of a stimulus is so difficult.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000081 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000081">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000747"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-09-13T20:38:41.979738Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Kinematics that positions a body part in a relatively fixed orientation.

An object may still move in relation to its environment (e.g. sliding and viscoelastic flexing).</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">placement</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>condition</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>position</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>state</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">placement kinematics</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Mother prevents access to breast by placing her arm across her nipple... She may also lie with breasts pressed to the ground or branch. Part of mother&apos;s weaning of her infant.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;relatively ornate changes in feather and wing placement&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000081"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Kinematics that positions a body part in a relatively fixed orientation.

An object may still move in relation to its environment (e.g. sliding and viscoelastic flexing).</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.198.1.79</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
