    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000094 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000094">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000147"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001519"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/kinetics"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-03-18T11:20:37.697715Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Kinetics that produces an audible, sonic wavelength.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>noise</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>sound production</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">sound kinetics</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Humans and other species often stop and listen if they need to detect a faint sound or to make a fine auditory discrimination. Orthopteran insects offer an extreme example. Because the auditory receptors of crickets are located on their forelegs, walking produces excitation of auditory receptors in the absence of sound and suppression of action potentials in response to sounds (Schildberger et al., 1988). Female crickets orienting to a male calling song pause frequently and change direction primarily during pauses.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;How a nerve comes to be sensitive to light, hardly concerns us more than how life itself first originated; but I may remark that several facts make me suspect that any sensitive nerve may be rendered sensitive to light, and likewise to those coarser vibrations of the air which produce sound.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The species we observe, mainly the successful survivors of different selection pressures, may be using structures to serve more than one function. The case of pharyngeal teeth which function both in sound production, and in feeding is a relevant example.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000094"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Kinetics that produces an audible, sonic wavelength.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000094"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>noise</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1967</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000094"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>sound production</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000094"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">sound kinetics</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000094"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Humans and other species often stop and listen if they need to detect a faint sound or to make a fine auditory discrimination. Orthopteran insects offer an extreme example. Because the auditory receptors of crickets are located on their forelegs, walking produces excitation of auditory receptors in the absence of sound and suppression of action potentials in response to sounds (Schildberger et al., 1988). Female crickets orienting to a male calling song pause frequently and change direction primarily during pauses.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/41.2.137</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000094"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;How a nerve comes to be sensitive to light, hardly concerns us more than how life itself first originated; but I may remark that several facts make me suspect that any sensitive nerve may be rendered sensitive to light, and likewise to those coarser vibrations of the air which produce sound.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000094"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The species we observe, mainly the successful survivors of different selection pressures, may be using structures to serve more than one function. The case of pharyngeal teeth which function both in sound production, and in feeding is a relevant example.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/BF01609520</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000438"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000114"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-03-27T10:38:02.585157Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Influence that signals quality.

There may be a verifiability cost in testing honesty or punishing dishonesty.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>advertising</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>costly signal</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>handicap</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>honesty</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>index signal</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">reliability influence</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...anal-fin fanning also may serve as a courtship display to attract females. In N. leptocephalus, the anterior anal-fin rays become bright white in breeding males compared to pale yellow or cream colored in nonbreeding males. White anal-fin rays and anal-fin fanning may exaggerate the appearance of a male releasing milt and perhaps stimulate in females a sensory bias for such males. Anal-fin fanning also may facilitate the release of chemical cues (e.g., milt, pheromones) into the spawning pit.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...costly signalling theory provides the ultimate, adaptive rationale for honest signalling, whereas the index hypothesis describes one proximate (and potentially very general) mechanism for achieving honesty.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The problem of reliability in signalling theory consists in explaining why signallers typically do not deceive receivers, given the incentive to do so. One solution to the problem appeals to the costs of signalling to explain reliability...

The key insight underlying costly signalling theory is that signal reliability can be maintained, despite conflicting sender and receiver interests, if dishonesty, while possible, is not profitable. The insight can apply, even if honest signals are free, because costly punishment of dishonesty counts as a kind of imposed signal cost.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Influence that signals quality.

There may be a verifiability cost in testing honesty or punishing dishonesty.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>advertising</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1967</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095"/>
