        <owl:annotatedTarget>handicap</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2012</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>honesty</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2012</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>index signal</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2012</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">reliability influence</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...anal-fin fanning also may serve as a courtship display to attract females. In N. leptocephalus, the anterior anal-fin rays become bright white in breeding males compared to pale yellow or cream colored in nonbreeding males. White anal-fin rays and anal-fin fanning may exaggerate the appearance of a male releasing milt and perhaps stimulate in females a sensory bias for such males. Anal-fin fanning also may facilitate the release of chemical cues (e.g., milt, pheromones) into the spawning pit.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...costly signalling theory provides the ultimate, adaptive rationale for honest signalling, whereas the index hypothesis describes one proximate (and potentially very general) mechanism for achieving honesty.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1098/rspb.2014.0876</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000095"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The problem of reliability in signalling theory consists in explaining why signallers typically do not deceive receivers, given the incentive to do so. One solution to the problem appeals to the costs of signalling to explain reliability...

The key insight underlying costly signalling theory is that signal reliability can be maintained, despite conflicting sender and receiver interests, if dishonesty, while possible, is not profitable. The insight can apply, even if honest signals are free, because costly punishment of dishonesty counts as a kind of imposed signal cost.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10539-011-9297-8</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000096 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000096">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000397"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002436"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-04-14T09:11:06.431784Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Routine pattern in which the order of a system is changed.

Order is considered to be negative entropy.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>rearranging</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>disarrangement</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>negentropy</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">rearrangement pattern</rdfs:label>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The primary function of the body-shake seems to be the removal of water drops from the bird&apos;s feathers, which is achieved by the centrifugal effect of the movements. It may also help to rearrange the feathers when they have been disordered (e.g. by preeening).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000096"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Routine pattern in which the order of a system is changed.

Order is considered to be negative entropy.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Different authors had suggested that the phenomenon of live and of evolution of living systems is related to the concept of increase in the order of the system; where order is considered to be negative entropy.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000096"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>rearranging</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000096"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>disarrangement</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1957</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&amp;context=wilson_bulletin</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000096"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>negentropy</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1984</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/0022-5193(84)90009-2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000096"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">rearrangement pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000096"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The primary function of the body-shake seems to be the removal of water drops from the bird&apos;s feathers, which is achieved by the centrifugal effect of the movements. It may also help to rearrange the feathers when they have been disordered (e.g. by preeening).&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000097 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000097">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000456"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-21T08:32:55.156149Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Activity that involves a fluid glandular secretion.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>exudation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym xml:lang="en">secretion</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">exudation activity</rdfs:label>
        <skos:closeMatch>NBO:0000041</skos:closeMatch>
        <skos:example>&quot;A second example concerns thermoregulation in Apis dorsata and A. florea... at higher ambient temperatures, nest cooling is enhanced by evaporation of water: some workers exude water droplets, while other bees fan their wings.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;DOLICHODERINAE (Sting vestigial; exude repugnatorial liquid from anal glands): Iridomyrmex detectus (F. Smith)&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Bodily secretions include saliva, wax, silk and even excrement, all of which can serve as effective glue for cementing external materials together.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000097"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Activity that involves a fluid glandular secretion.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&amp;context=wilson_bulletin</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Instead of spraying acid secretions when disturbed, these species exude from the anal glands fluid droplets having the scent of rancid butter.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000097"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>exudation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1957</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&amp;context=wilson_bulletin</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000097"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">secretion</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2002</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000097"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">exudation activity</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000097"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;A second example concerns thermoregulation in Apis dorsata and A. florea... at higher ambient temperatures, nest cooling is enhanced by evaporation of water: some workers exude water droplets, while other bees fan their wings.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/beheco/12.5.534</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000097"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;DOLICHODERINAE (Sting vestigial; exude repugnatorial liquid from anal glands): Iridomyrmex detectus (F. Smith)&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&amp;context=wilson_bulletin</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000097"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Bodily secretions include saliva, wax, silk and even excrement, all of which can serve as effective glue for cementing external materials together.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2021.09.082</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000098 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000098">
