        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The genera Lingula and Glottidia possess antero-posteriorly elongated shells, and live within mucus-lined burrows in soft sediment, with the long pedicles anchored at the bottom of their burrows... After burrowing downward for a while, they curve upward and continue burrowing until they emerge from the substrate with the anterior region of the shell. The valves perform a variety of gaping and shearing movements that are adaptive in displacing the sediment.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/j.1365-3121.1991.tb00141.x</dc:source>
