    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000124 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000124">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000093"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000085"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000188"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000026"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-09-17T19:48:54.590671Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Traversing-gait that lifts an appendage and accelerates it with respect to the body, before repositioning it on the ground ready to push and accelerate the body in the desired direction.

The lift phase is the swing, the ground phase is the stance, and the sum of all the pushing appendages over a complete locomotor cycle is the stride (in bipeds this tends to equal a full cycle in one leg, although it is not so simple in multipeds). The relative fraction of a stride period when a particular limb is in the stance phase is defined as that limb’s duty factor.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">step</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>stepping</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>stride</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>stepping gait</rdfs:label>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;When an animal moves, independently from the gait and speed, the limbs push on the ground accelerating the body in the desired direction, during the so called stance phase, then the limbs must be repositioned to take a step forward, leaving the ground and accelerating with respect to the body, in the course of the swing phase. During locomotion, the forces exerted by one limb on the ground rise and fall during the stance phase (when the foot is on the ground), and are zero when the limb is in the swing phase, but the average vertical force exerted by the sum of the limb over a complete locomotor cycle (stride) must be equal to the body weight. It follows that if the limbs are kept on the ground for a shorter period of time, larger forces will be exerted on each limb.

The relative fraction of a stride period when a particular limb is in the stance phase is defined as the limb’s duty factor (Df).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000124"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Traversing-gait that lifts an appendage and accelerates it with respect to the body, before repositioning it on the ground ready to push and accelerate the body in the desired direction.

The lift phase is the swing, the ground phase is the stance, and the sum of all the pushing appendages over a complete locomotor cycle is the stride (in bipeds this tends to equal a full cycle in one leg, although it is not so simple in multipeds). The relative fraction of a stride period when a particular limb is in the stance phase is defined as that limb’s duty factor.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.13130/biancardi-carlo-massimo_phd2012-01-31</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000124"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">step</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1977</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1152/ajpregu.1977.233.5.R243</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000124"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>stepping</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1986</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.120.1.283</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000124"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>stride</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1967</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000124"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>stepping gait</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000124"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;When an animal moves, independently from the gait and speed, the limbs push on the ground accelerating the body in the desired direction, during the so called stance phase, then the limbs must be repositioned to take a step forward, leaving the ground and accelerating with respect to the body, in the course of the swing phase. During locomotion, the forces exerted by one limb on the ground rise and fall during the stance phase (when the foot is on the ground), and are zero when the limb is in the swing phase, but the average vertical force exerted by the sum of the limb over a complete locomotor cycle (stride) must be equal to the body weight. It follows that if the limbs are kept on the ground for a shorter period of time, larger forces will be exerted on each limb.

The relative fraction of a stride period when a particular limb is in the stance phase is defined as the limb’s duty factor (Df).&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.13130/biancardi-carlo-massimo_phd2012-01-31</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000125 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000125">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000446"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000690"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-05-26T14:42:21.941692Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Performance which contains atomic elements that inherently increase attention and response, and prevent habituation.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>feature signal</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">feature performance</rdfs:label>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...rough auditory percepts are analogous to flickering phenomena in the visual modality...
...subjective reports linearly follow stimulus frequency above the subjective transition from discrete (rough) to continuous (pitch) percepts (~130 Hz), but not below. Instead, sounds in the roughness range induce highly aversive, buzzing percepts that cannot be accounted for merely by stimulus energy.
...the negative percept induced by rough sounds such as dissonant intervals, alarm sounds or annoying vocal effects (e.g. vocal fry) might result from their capacity to massively, exogenously hijack brain networks involved in aversion and pain processing.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000125"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Performance which contains atomic elements that inherently increase attention and response, and prevent habituation.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1007/s10988-025-09438-8</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The alarm calls of many species contain acoustic features that are inherently attention-grabbing or frightening, and this may also prompt immediate response to unfamiliar calls (…). Many mobbing, distress, “panic,” or general alarm calls, for example, contain elements that are harsh, abrupt, broadband, or nonlinear. These acoustic features tend to be honest signals of caller distress, increase listener attention and response, and prevent habituation (…).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000125"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>feature signal</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10988-025-09438-8</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...features tend to be honest signals...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000125"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">feature performance</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000125"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...rough auditory percepts are analogous to flickering phenomena in the visual modality...
...subjective reports linearly follow stimulus frequency above the subjective transition from discrete (rough) to continuous (pitch) percepts (~130 Hz), but not below. Instead, sounds in the roughness range induce highly aversive, buzzing percepts that cannot be accounted for merely by stimulus energy.
...the negative percept induced by rough sounds such as dissonant intervals, alarm sounds or annoying vocal effects (e.g. vocal fry) might result from their capacity to massively, exogenously hijack brain networks involved in aversion and pain processing.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1038/s41467-019-11626-7</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000126 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000126">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000017"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002323"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-09-19T20:04:57.601329Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Pattern that involves particular, cyclical movements, characteristic of a limited range of speeds in which momentum is conserved across repeated cycles.

It is described by quantities of which one or more change discontinuously at transitions to other gaits.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym xml:lang="en">gait</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>kinematics</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>gait pattern</rdfs:label>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Due to the periodical characteristics of a gait, where a sequence of footfalls is repeated in time, the gait pattern is summarized by phase shifts of footfalls that can be plotted as points on a trigonometric circumference.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000126"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Pattern that involves particular, cyclical movements, characteristic of a limited range of speeds in which momentum is conserved across repeated cycles.

It is described by quantities of which one or more change discontinuously at transitions to other gaits.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1093/icb/icac125</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Kinematic (the study of themovement of body parts) and kinetic (the study of energetic and force patterns) analysis techniques are often used to identify functional differences in “gaits,” defined as particular cyclical pattern of movement characteristic of a limited range of speeds in which momentum is conserved across repeated cycles, described by quantities of which one or more change discontinuously at transitions to other gaits.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000126"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">gait</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000126"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>kinematics</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1991</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.158.1.369</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...few investigations have correlated kinematics (i.e. description of stepping patterns) with the actual kinetics of locomotion.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000126"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>gait pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1986</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.120.1.283</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000126"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Due to the periodical characteristics of a gait, where a sequence of footfalls is repeated in time, the gait pattern is summarized by phase shifts of footfalls that can be plotted as points on a trigonometric circumference.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.13130/biancardi-carlo-massimo_phd2012-01-31</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000127 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000127">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000739"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000151"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-07-12T10:22:16.535411Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Adducting in which a pectoral fin is returning to the body.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">pectoral-fin adducting</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;If a faster start is required the head initiates lateral trunk un-dulations in the manner typical for most fishes, and the spread pectoral fins are clapped against the sides in unison.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Such forms that have comparatively narrow pectorals used to this end, as Abudefduf and Teuthus, may be, in one sense, almost thought of as rowing their way along, in that they bring the fin forward almost edgewise and force it back broadside.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000127"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Adducting in which a pectoral fin is returning to the body.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1242/jeb.204.8.1459</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Pectoral fin-beat cycles started with the fins pressed against the body (maximally adducted), moving away (abducting) from the body to some maximum point of excursion...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000127"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">pectoral-fin adducting</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2001</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.204.8.1459</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Pectoral fin-beat cycles started with the fins pressed against the body (maximally adducted), moving away... and returning (adducting) to the original position.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000127"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;If a faster start is required the head initiates lateral trunk un-dulations in the manner typical for most fishes, and the spread pectoral fins are clapped against the sides in unison.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000127"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Such forms that have comparatively narrow pectorals used to this end, as Abudefduf and Teuthus, may be, in one sense, almost thought of as rowing their way along, in that they bring the fin forward almost edgewise and force it back broadside.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000126"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000026"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-09-19T20:25:01.141445Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Gait pattern in which an organism with multiple appendages performs a stroke by allowing some phase delay between adjacent appendages.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>leg-density wave</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>metachronal beat</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>metachronal wave</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>locomotory wave</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym xml:lang="en">metachronal gait</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>metachronal pattern</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Scolopocryptops rubiginous... possess various modes of locomotion that use retrograde waves, direct waves, and a combination of both that we will call splitting waves.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The resulting gait of a sideways-walking crab, such as Uca pugnax, has been best described as an &apos;approximate&apos; alternating tetrapod, with tendencies for metachronal waves.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Most metachronally swimming animals use an antiplectic pattern, in which the power stroke direction is opposite to the appendage wave direction.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Gait pattern in which an organism with multiple appendages performs a stroke by allowing some phase delay between adjacent appendages.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>https://search.proquest.com/openview/28072abf0dcabdc270c0235a6fb16211/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Metachronal swimming or metachronal rowing is a type of locomotion that arises when an organism with multiple appendages (legs, pleopods, ctene plates, etc.) performs a stroke by allowing some phase delay between adjacent appendages.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>leg-density wave</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2022</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1088/1748-3190/ac482d</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>metachronal beat</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1983</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3354/meps011089</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>metachronal wave</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1983</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3354/meps011089</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>locomotory wave</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2022</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1088/1748-3190/ac482d</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">metachronal gait</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1993</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/BF00197760</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>metachronal pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Scolopocryptops rubiginous... possess various modes of locomotion that use retrograde waves, direct waves, and a combination of both that we will call splitting waves.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1088/1748-3190/ac482d</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The resulting gait of a sideways-walking crab, such as Uca pugnax, has been best described as an &apos;approximate&apos; alternating tetrapod, with tendencies for metachronal waves.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.130.1.155</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000128"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Most metachronally swimming animals use an antiplectic pattern, in which the power stroke direction is opposite to the appendage wave direction.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/28072abf0dcabdc270c0235a6fb16211/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000129 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000129">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000739"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000152"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-07-12T10:49:51.455687Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Adducting which involves a pelvic fin.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">pelvic-fin adducting</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;In the family Rajidae... The pelvics have a special function when the skate is resting on the bottom; they operate to push the animal forward by kicking back much as though they were feet. Frequently in rising the members of this family give a single violent backward thrust, so launching themselves on their way.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000129"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Adducting which involves a pelvic fin.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000129"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">pelvic-fin adducting</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000129"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In the family Rajidae... The pelvics have a special function when the skate is resting on the bottom; they operate to push the animal forward by kicking back much as though they were feet. Frequently in rising the members of this family give a single violent backward thrust, so launching themselves on their way.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000130 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000130">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000126"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000441"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-09-22T10:02:04.774121Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Gait pattern which describes the cyclical order and combinations of footfalls.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">sequential characteristics</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>The  &apos;sequential&apos; gait grouping has more recently been supplemented by energy recovery terms (&apos;vaulting&apos;, &apos;bouncing&apos;, and &apos;skipping&apos;). Terms like &apos;running&apos; and &apos;symmetrical&apos; are now insufficient to exclusively define a gait, but retain an inclusive meaning.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>sequence pattern</rdfs:label>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Hence, the walk can be categorized as a symmetrical, even four-beat lateral sequence “singlefoot” gait.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000130"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Gait pattern which describes the cyclical order and combinations of footfalls.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.2307/1379571</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;A gait is an accustomed, cyclic manner of moving in terrestrial locomotion. Although actions of the head, spine, and tail may be gait characteristics, this study is limited to the timing of the footfalls, the durations of the contacts that the feet make with the ground, and the sequences of the different patterns of support made by the feet in combination. There are two principal classes of gaits, symmetrical and asymmetrical. The former has the footfalls of a pair of feet evenly spaced in time.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000130"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">sequential characteristics</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1968</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/html/tr/AD0675256/</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000130"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>sequence pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2000</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1023/A:1005565304671</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000130"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Hence, the walk can be categorized as a symmetrical, even four-beat lateral sequence “singlefoot” gait.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3390/ani13162557</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


