    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000195 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000195">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000086"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000192"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000251"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000173"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/locomotion_realizing_regime"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-09-26T19:21:41.35768Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Interfacing-regime that involves falling, with a mechanism to increase drag.

Associated with descent at a sharp angle (i.e., 45-90° from horizontal). Ballooning arthropods also parachute, but they are most likely to fall free during wind, resulting in notable horizontal movement such that their angle of descent may often meet the criterion for gliding; so called &quot;windblown&quot; parachuting.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>parachute</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">parachuting</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>parachute regime</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...a patagium attached the body section... in... the ‘Flying’ Gecko, Ptychozoon kuhli... automatically deploys upon the reptile launching into the air...

...Rhacophorus, are a group of tree frogs that have strong webbing located between their digits.

...the ‘flying’ snakes belonging to the genus group Chrysopelea... prominent body movements are utilized in air rather than simply relying on a fixed wing arrangement.

All the reptile examples detailed above really only exhibit... a parachuting aerial mode, reducing the sinking speed of the animal slightly.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Aequidens latfrons...
After the parents have ejected the young over the pit these sink down slowly with turning movements like maple seeds. The yolk is apparently heavier than the tail which brakes the sinking movement.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000195"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Interfacing-regime that involves falling, with a mechanism to increase drag.

Associated with descent at a sharp angle (i.e., 45-90° from horizontal). Ballooning arthropods also parachute, but they are most likely to fall free during wind, resulting in notable horizontal movement such that their angle of descent may often meet the criterion for gliding; so called &quot;windblown&quot; parachuting.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1111/j.1744-7429.2000.tb00506.x</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;PARACHUTE. - A falling or jumping organism having behavioral or morphological mechanisms to increase drag.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000195"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>parachute</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
