        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>scoot gait</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000241 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000241">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000126"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0013701"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-04T10:24:19.26324Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Gait pattern which has both sliding and static contact, and the head-to-tail distance changes as a convoluted region of the body provides a static anchoring region with subsequent straightening that slides the body forward.

In all types of concertina locomotion, the convolutions that anchor the snake via static contact are created from anterior to posterior.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">concertina</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>concertina pattern</rdfs:label>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;On horizontal and uphill perches snakes performed a variant of concertina locomotion with periodic stopping and gripping.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000241"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Gait pattern which has both sliding and static contact, and the head-to-tail distance changes as a convoluted region of the body provides a static anchoring region with subsequent straightening that slides the body forward.

In all types of concertina locomotion, the convolutions that anchor the snake via static contact are created from anterior to posterior.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icaa017</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;During concertina locomotion snakes have both sliding and static contact, and the head-to-tail distance changes as a convoluted region of the body provides a static anchoring region with subsequent straightening that slides the body forward.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000241"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">concertina</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2000</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/j.1744-7429.2000.tb00506.x</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000241"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>concertina pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000241"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;On horizontal and uphill perches snakes performed a variant of concertina locomotion with periodic stopping and gripping.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.009050</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


