        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">cognition context</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000270"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Animals also exhibit emotional states, like states of heightened anxiety or depression, as well as cognitive states, for example, in the contexts of attention or working memory.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1534/genetics.120.303539</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000270"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In order to provide a more rigorous foundation for research and to advance our understanding of the interrelation between tool use and cognition, we suggest the adoption of Fragaszy and Mangalam’s (2018) tooling framework, which is characterized by the creation of a body-plus-object system that manages a mechanical interface between tool and surface. Tooling is limited to a narrower suite of behaviors than tool use, which might facilitate its neurocognitive investigation. Indeed, evidence in the literature indicates that tooling has distinct neurocognitive underpinnings not shared by other activities typically classified as tool use, at least in primates.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/tops.12554</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000271 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000271">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000078"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-07T08:06:22.076385Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Activity which uses an artifact to modify an environment.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>technic</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">technical enactivism</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">technic activity</rdfs:label>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Venturing a Pragmatist Interpretation of Nonhuman Animals’ Technical Capacities:

Those problematic and particular situations that are solved with an active adaptation can lead to different kinds of solutions, some of which can be achieved through modification of habits... In some cases, however, animals can use mediating objects to find a solution for the problematic situation. Of course, not every mediating object used by an agent is necessarily an artifact... An artifact could be defined as an extreme case of a mediating object created or used to solve a problematic situation in an efficient way. Sometimes the object already exists, and it is picked out from among other natural objects for its special structure (naturfacts) – a structure that is identified by the animal for its outstanding features for solving the problematic situation... Yet, in other cases, the object does not exist before, and the agent creates it, either by modifying natural objects, or by combining and assembling different natural objects. The structure of the object allows the animal to deal with the problematic situation, transforming it into a non-problematic one.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000271"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Activity which uses an artifact to modify an environment.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.4000/ejpap.2324</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000271"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>technic</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2021</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.4000/ejpap.2324</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000271"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">technical enactivism</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2021</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.4000/ejpap.2324</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000271"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">technic activity</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000271"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Venturing a Pragmatist Interpretation of Nonhuman Animals’ Technical Capacities:

Those problematic and particular situations that are solved with an active adaptation can lead to different kinds of solutions, some of which can be achieved through modification of habits... In some cases, however, animals can use mediating objects to find a solution for the problematic situation. Of course, not every mediating object used by an agent is necessarily an artifact... An artifact could be defined as an extreme case of a mediating object created or used to solve a problematic situation in an efficient way. Sometimes the object already exists, and it is picked out from among other natural objects for its special structure (naturfacts) – a structure that is identified by the animal for its outstanding features for solving the problematic situation... Yet, in other cases, the object does not exist before, and the agent creates it, either by modifying natural objects, or by combining and assembling different natural objects. The structure of the object allows the animal to deal with the problematic situation, transforming it into a non-problematic one.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.4000/ejpap.2324</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000272 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000272">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000271"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000096"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/technical_realizing_routine"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-07T08:07:26.13089Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Technic in which an animal&apos;s activity creates functional architecture, using characteristic materials and techniques, and involving labour and other costs.

Costs may be significant for building, maintenance, or defense. Structures can become very complex, exemplifying the &apos;extended genotype&apos; concept, and even suggesting well developed cognitive mechanisms. Functions include feeding, sheltering, socialising and signaling. There may also be significant ecosystem (niche) and inter-generational (even evolutionary) impacts.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>building</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">construction</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>rebuilding</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>remodelling</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>reshaping</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>maintaining</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">construction technic</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...the spawning pit or trough creates a pocket of slack water...  a male N. micropogon continually reshapes the original trough.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;As in the cells of the hive-bee, so here, the three plane surfaces in any one cell necessarily enter into the construction of three adjoining cells. It is obvious that the Melipona saves wax by this manner of building; for the flat walls between the adjoining cells are not double, but are of the same thickness as the outer spherical portions, and yet each flat portion forms a part of two cells.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Cases of animal architecture should therefore be evolutionarily shaped to maximize the adaptive benefi ts to the architect, devalued by any costs the architect incurs in constructing, maintaining, and defending its architecture. The extended phenotype concept thus sheds essential light on when architecture will be evolutionarily favored.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000272"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Technic in which an animal&apos;s activity creates functional architecture, using characteristic materials and techniques, and involving labour and other costs.

Costs may be significant for building, maintenance, or defense. Structures can become very complex, exemplifying the &apos;extended genotype&apos; concept, and even suggesting well developed cognitive mechanisms. Functions include feeding, sheltering, socialising and signaling. There may also be significant ecosystem (niche) and inter-generational (even evolutionary) impacts.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2021.09.082</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000272"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>building</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
