    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000294 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000294">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000292"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_00010483"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-08T07:08:00.004332Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Refuge which shields from harmful environments.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>obstruction</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">obstructive cover</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">shelter</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>shield</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>shelter refuge</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000886"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;...a case of &quot;resource sharing&quot; where diurnal and nocturnal water-column foragers replace one another in resting shelters over the 24 hour cycle...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;...increased habitat complexity act to reduce predation by placing more visual obstructions in the path opf predators thus increasing the ability of mobile prey to hide, evade and escape. Therefore, gobies on D. gemmacea could take advantage of its complex multiple-branching structure to confuse predators...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Thalassoma cupido...
When attacked, the wrasses quickly dispersed, seeking refuge under the algae where they remained for about 30 seconds.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...flow-refuging behaviour in wave-swept habitats.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;For this analysis, vegetation types were classified as ‘grazing’, which were areas dominated by open grassland, ‘cover’, which were areas dominated by trees or shrubs, and ‘grazing-cover’, which were areas with a mixture of grassland and discrete patches of refuge habitat, either woodland edges (obstructive cover) or flooded pans (protective cover into which herbivores can retreat when attacked).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Hermit crabs are unique animals for locomotion studies. They are specialized to carry snail shells. The addition of this load obviously reduces the crab&apos;s speed and manoeuvrability, and increases the energy used for transport, while it provides the advantage of protection from predators and environmental extremes.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Large boulders, smaller rocks, cliffs, crevices, caves, coral outcroppings, and lush algal meadows provide adequate shelter not only for spawning labrids, but also for piscivores and potential egg predators.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000294"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Refuge which shields from harmful environments.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>http://researcharchive.vuw.ac.nz/handle/10063/9442</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Some nest sites may be less prone to predation and shield the parents or eggs from harmful environments.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
