The nearer the female approaches oestrus the more inclined she is to stand against the attacks of the male.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000306"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;If the predator does not respond to the aposematic signal, they can deliver a few effective bites, then cover up their heads and withstand a brief attack while they wait for the envenomation to repel the attacker.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000306"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;When a fish has been pushed back as far as the centre of its territory, it usually posts itself with the hind part on the bottom, the head lifted up and directed against the intruder. At this point it apparently intends to give no more ground away, and it often succeeds in keeping this last position, particularly when it has some shelter at its back.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
