    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000319 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000319">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000148"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_activity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-08-20T14:31:28.809775Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>equilibrium</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Perception that adequately stabilises locomotor and postural behaviour.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">balancing</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>counterbalancing</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>balance perception</rdfs:label>
        <skos:closeMatch>NBO:0000318</skos:closeMatch>
        <skos:example>&quot;Acanthopterygians that have the bases of the pectorals and pelvics usually placed rather close together and are comparatively long bodied also usually have the tips of the dorsal and anal lobate. These are frequently brought “hard over” to check movement. The genera Micropterus, Epinephelus and Micteroperca quite commonly employ the tail in this manner, virtually forming a sea anchor. Any tendency caused by this to bring about undesirable deflection to one side is counterbalanced by an appropriate pectoral movement. Lepomis and Micropterus at times curve their tails to one side and the dorsal and anal lobes to the other, thus checking movement and avoiding deflection.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;C. compressus uses a basic alternating tripod gait... The crab&apos;s abdomen and the centre of gravity of the mollusc shell are displaced to the right of the crab&apos;s midline. In order to maintain balance, the crab has compensated behaviourally and morphologically. The behavioural change is the alignment of the legs. The left legs are extended and the right retracted.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;In fishes and other animals that live under water the (locomotory) movements are more closely connected with the problem of maintaining the equilibrium than in animals living on land.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000319"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasBroadSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>equilibrium</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000319"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Perception that adequately stabilises locomotor and postural behaviour.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1023/A:1005565304671</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000319"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">balancing</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000319"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>counterbalancing</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000319"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>balance perception</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000319"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Acanthopterygians that have the bases of the pectorals and pelvics usually placed rather close together and are comparatively long bodied also usually have the tips of the dorsal and anal lobate. These are frequently brought “hard over” to check movement. The genera Micropterus, Epinephelus and Micteroperca quite commonly employ the tail in this manner, virtually forming a sea anchor. Any tendency caused by this to bring about undesirable deflection to one side is counterbalanced by an appropriate pectoral movement. Lepomis and Micropterus at times curve their tails to one side and the dorsal and anal lobes to the other, thus checking movement and avoiding deflection.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000319"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;C. compressus uses a basic alternating tripod gait... The crab&apos;s abdomen and the centre of gravity of the mollusc shell are displaced to the right of the crab&apos;s midline. In order to maintain balance, the crab has compensated behaviourally and morphologically. The behavioural change is the alignment of the legs. The left legs are extended and the right retracted.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.120.1.283</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000319"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In fishes and other animals that live under water the (locomotory) movements are more closely connected with the problem of maintaining the equilibrium than in animals living on land.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000320 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000320">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000118"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="genid2363"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="genid2365"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="genid2367"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="genid2369"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="genid2371"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="genid2373"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="genid2375"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-08-31T15:04:15.322717Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Ethotype which involves a display, varying by species, involving ritualized sequences of comfort movements.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">comfort related</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>This is a potential parent category for a litany of rituals.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>comfort-related ethotype</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;In the Common Eider and King Eider, one of the commonest pre-copulatory sequences is Bathe + Wing-flap + Shake; this may be repeated many times, along with other displays, before the male finally mounts.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The White-winged Scoter male gives movements resembling Bill-dip associated with Drink and Preen-behind-wing.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The male Andean Goose has two displays which appear to have been derived from oiling. In the first, the head is suddenly thrown backward and the crown is rolled on the middle of the back with a jerky motion. At other times, the male approaches the female with feathers depressed all over the body; on reaching her he suddenly erects all the body feathers and rolls the chin sideways over the breast.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The only instance I recorded of head-flicks in the pre-copulation situation was in a male Red-crested Pochard. Dipping the bill to the water several times was followed by Head-flick and Preen-dorsally, the whole sequence being repeated many times before mounting occurred.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
