    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000320"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>This is a potential parent category for a litany of rituals.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000320"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>comfort-related ethotype</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000320"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In the Common Eider and King Eider, one of the commonest pre-copulatory sequences is Bathe + Wing-flap + Shake; this may be repeated many times, along with other displays, before the male finally mounts.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000320"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The White-winged Scoter male gives movements resembling Bill-dip associated with Drink and Preen-behind-wing.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000320"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The male Andean Goose has two displays which appear to have been derived from oiling. In the first, the head is suddenly thrown backward and the crown is rolled on the middle of the back with a jerky motion. At other times, the male approaches the female with feathers depressed all over the body; on reaching her he suddenly erects all the body feathers and rolls the chin sideways over the breast.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000320"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The only instance I recorded of head-flicks in the pre-copulation situation was in a male Red-crested Pochard. Dipping the bill to the water several times was followed by Head-flick and Preen-dorsally, the whole sequence being repeated many times before mounting occurred.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000321 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000321">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000472"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000411"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-08-26T14:16:12.839615Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Courtship in which a suitor gathers with others performing displays around a potential mate.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">courting party behaviour</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>social courtship</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Head-shakes commonly occur in duck courting parties... Certain displays are normally preceded or followed by a head-shake; for example the sequence: Head-shake + Head-flick + Head-throw is common in the Greater Scaup, Tufted Duck, and Southern Pochard.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;JOHNSGARD records Wing-flaps in courting parties of Common and Barrow&apos;s Goldeneyes, Hooded Merganser, Smew, Red-breasted Merganser and Goosander, stressing that the movement is especially well developed in the Bufflehead.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The Belly-preen... may be given during courting party activity or when a drake is alone beside his female... I have seen a male move away from courting party on the water to run out on the nearby shore, perform the Shake + Preen laterally to the female, and then rush back into the water to join the group.&quot;&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000321"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Courtship in which a suitor gathers with others performing displays around a potential mate.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000321"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">courting party behaviour</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...courting party behaviour
...courting party activity
...courting party situation
...courting party display...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000321"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>social courtship</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000321"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Head-shakes commonly occur in duck courting parties... Certain displays are normally preceded or followed by a head-shake; for example the sequence: Head-shake + Head-flick + Head-throw is common in the Greater Scaup, Tufted Duck, and Southern Pochard.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000321"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;JOHNSGARD records Wing-flaps in courting parties of Common and Barrow&apos;s Goldeneyes, Hooded Merganser, Smew, Red-breasted Merganser and Goosander, stressing that the movement is especially well developed in the Bufflehead.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000321"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The Belly-preen... may be given during courting party activity or when a drake is alone beside his female... I have seen a male move away from courting party on the water to run out on the nearby shore, perform the Shake + Preen laterally to the female, and then rush back into the water to join the group.&quot;&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000322 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000322">
