    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000751"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000077"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0001032"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-15T15:09:54.34483Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Context in which the integration of sensory information structures subsequent behavior.

In a stimulus-response loop each movement may generate a new sensory stimulus for the animal that, itself, serves as a trigger for the next movement in the chain.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">perceiving</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>perception context</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Considering how flexible thin wax is, I do not see that there is any difficulty in the bees, whilst at work on the two sides of a strip of wax, perceiving when they have gnawed the wax away to the proper thinness, and then stopping their work.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Tinbergen (1953), using models, showed that in Herring Gulls the red spot on the parent&apos;s bill is the primary releaser of the pecking response of the chick. Neither the background color of the bill or head color are releasers; in fact, the presence of the head is not even necessary.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:relatedMatch>NBO:0000308</skos:relatedMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...their fractal scale of perception (i.e., what may be a distinct space or barrier to one organism may be perceived as a continuum to another larger or differently adapted organism)...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Given that corals may be sensing chromaticity differences from either solar or lunar irradiance, the dynamics of specific wavelengths may have a significant effect on how corals perceive spectral shifts.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;When the agent faces a problematic situation, in the first instance, it only perceives and acts, but if the action does not solve the situation, then the normal situation becomes an occasion for inquiry.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;‘A tool is an extension of the hand in both a physical and a perceptual sense’...

...we treat what an organism perceives in the environment as information.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Context in which the integration of sensory information structures subsequent behavior.

In a stimulus-response loop each movement may generate a new sensory stimulus for the animal that, itself, serves as a trigger for the next movement in the chain.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The continuous integration of sensory information is necessary to structure the behavior...

...in a stimulus-response loop each movement generates a new sensory stimulus for the animal that, itself, serves as a trigger for the next movement in the chain.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">perceiving</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>perception context</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Considering how flexible thin wax is, I do not see that there is any difficulty in the bees, whilst at work on the two sides of a strip of wax, perceiving when they have gnawed the wax away to the proper thinness, and then stopping their work.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Tinbergen (1953), using models, showed that in Herring Gulls the red spot on the parent&apos;s bill is the primary releaser of the pecking response of the chick. Neither the background color of the bill or head color are releasers; in fact, the presence of the head is not even necessary.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/4083155</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...their fractal scale of perception (i.e., what may be a distinct space or barrier to one organism may be perceived as a continuum to another larger or differently adapted organism)...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/j.1744-7429.2000.tb00506.x</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Given that corals may be sensing chromaticity differences from either solar or lunar irradiance, the dynamics of specific wavelengths may have a significant effect on how corals perceive spectral shifts.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/bc60659126ef5c0172c2392d485b0c9b/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;When the agent faces a problematic situation, in the first instance, it only perceives and acts, but if the action does not solve the situation, then the normal situation becomes an occasion for inquiry.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.4000/ejpap.2324</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000335"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;‘A tool is an extension of the hand in both a physical and a perceptual sense’...

...we treat what an organism perceives in the environment as information.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.anbehav.2007.09.028</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000336 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000336">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000118"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000324"/>
