    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000341"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="genid2598"/>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_realization"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-21T08:35:07.758569Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Maintenance that is related to consumption of food.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">feeding</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>feed maintenance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000846"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;Although lying was not a common feeding posture, when chimpanzees fed while lying the food item was most often fruit or insects.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:relatedMatch>NBO:0000079</skos:relatedMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Since feeding is essential for survival, it impacts numerous processes and decisions throughout an animal’s life. In C. elegans, feeding controls not only growth rate, body size, fat accumulation, brood size, and lifespan, but also behaviors and decisions such as various forms of taxis, dauer decision, and egg-laying. In most cases, sensory perception of food is integrated into the worm’s metabolic status and influences the animal’s behavioral output to maximize its fitness.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;What a struggle between the several kinds of trees must here have gone on during long centuries, each annually scattering its seeds by the thousand; what war between insect and insect—between insects, snails, and other animals with birds and beasts of prey—all striving to increase, and all feeding on each other or on the trees or their seeds and seedlings, or on the other plants which first clothed the ground and thus checked the growth of the trees!&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Restriction rdf:nodeID="genid2598">
        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000397"/>
    </owl:Restriction>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid2598"/>
        <rdfs:comment>&apos;Bout&apos; used here as a synonym for &apos;routine&apos;.</rdfs:comment>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;However, if both the upright and on-the-side approaches occur in the same feeding bout the on-the-side technique more often appears first and subsequently is replaced by the upright method.&quot;

http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Maintenance that is related to consumption of food.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1089/zeb.2012.0861</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Feeding: Behaviors related to consumption of food;&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">feeding</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>feed maintenance</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Although lying was not a common feeding posture, when chimpanzees fed while lying the food item was most often fruit or insects.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1002/ajpa.1330870108</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Since feeding is essential for survival, it impacts numerous processes and decisions throughout an animal’s life. In C. elegans, feeding controls not only growth rate, body size, fat accumulation, brood size, and lifespan, but also behaviors and decisions such as various forms of taxis, dauer decision, and egg-laying. In most cases, sensory perception of food is integrated into the worm’s metabolic status and influences the animal’s behavioral output to maximize its fitness.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1534/genetics.120.303539</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;What a struggle between the several kinds of trees must here have gone on during long centuries, each annually scattering its seeds by the thousand; what war between insect and insect—between insects, snails, and other animals with birds and beasts of prey—all striving to increase, and all feeding on each other or on the trees or their seeds and seedlings, or on the other plants which first clothed the ground and thus checked the growth of the trees!&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000301"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-21T08:35:23.624748Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Feed which conceals and protects food.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>caching</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>hoarding</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>storage</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">store</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>storage feed</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000913"/>
        <skos:broadMatch>NBO:0020068</skos:broadMatch>
        <skos:example>&quot;...Tachytes nigra generally makes its own burrow and stores it with paralysed prey for its own larvæ to feed on...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Clark’s nutcrackers are often considered to be the animal champions of long-term spatial memory and can retrieve cached food hordes at least 285 days after they were stashed but a fair degree of forgetting starts to set in after 183 days.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Honeybees, for example, build hexagonal beeswax combs, in which they store honey and raise future generations.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Feed which conceals and protects food.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1016/j.beproc.2014.10.001</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Food caching, also labeled food storing or food hoarding, is a common behavior in many species of birds and mammals, with the general purpose of concealment and protection of food.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>caching</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2015</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.beproc.2014.10.001</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>hoarding</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2015</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.beproc.2014.10.001</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>storage</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2015</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.beproc.2014.10.001</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">store</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>storage feed</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...Tachytes nigra generally makes its own burrow and stores it with paralysed prey for its own larvæ to feed on...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Clark’s nutcrackers are often considered to be the animal champions of long-term spatial memory and can retrieve cached food hordes at least 285 days after they were stashed but a fair degree of forgetting starts to set in after 183 days.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10071-014-0761-0</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000349"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Honeybees, for example, build hexagonal beeswax combs, in which they store honey and raise future generations.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2021.09.082</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000350 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000350">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000348"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000498"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-21T08:35:36.583825Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Feed that counters a food hazard by using a careful risk minimization technique.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">handling</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym xml:lang="en">prey handling</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">handle feed</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Ictalurid catfish possess locking spines which make them dangerous to gape-limited predators such as herons and grebes, but not to ospreys (Pandion haliaetus) which dismember prey. Herons and grebes handled ictalurids longer than prey without locking spines, but osprey did not.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Some prey are risky to eat, as defensive structures of prey (e.g., spines, thorns, claws) may injure or kill a predator during ingestion. But predators may counter these defenses with a variety of handling behaviours (e.g., dismemberment, crushing, stabbing) which serve to kill or incpacitate prey.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The derived venom delivery systems of elapids and viperids most likely evolved first as an adaptation to aid in the capture, handling, and digestion of prey.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000350"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Feed that counters a food hazard by using a careful risk minimization technique.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/3565418</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000350"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">handling</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1989</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/3565418</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000350"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">prey handling</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1989</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/3565418</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000350"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">handle feed</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000350"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Ictalurid catfish possess locking spines which make them dangerous to gape-limited predators such as herons and grebes, but not to ospreys (Pandion haliaetus) which dismember prey. Herons and grebes handled ictalurids longer than prey without locking spines, but osprey did not.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/3565418</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000350"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Some prey are risky to eat, as defensive structures of prey (e.g., spines, thorns, claws) may injure or kill a predator during ingestion. But predators may counter these defenses with a variety of handling behaviours (e.g., dismemberment, crushing, stabbing) which serve to kill or incpacitate prey.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/3565418</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
