    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000353"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000907"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ECOCORE_00000148"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-21T08:37:28.561529Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Forage that involves a predatory mode.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">hunting</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>predation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>hunt forage</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;The most significant characteristic of hunting behavior in Badis badis is that catching and eating each prey produces steadily increasing parsimony of behavior in capturing the next, if this is done at once.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:narrowMatch>NBO:0009003</skos:narrowMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Predation is a pervasive and unforgiving selection pressure on prey populations. A large and diverse body of research has explored the impacts of predation on the life history, morphology, physiology and behaviour of prey on both ecological and evolutionary time scales. Predators can exert adverse effects on their prey either directly by killing them or indirectly by instilling ‘fear’ in them, which induces behavioural, physiological and neurobiological costs on individuals attempting to avoid predation.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Predators can affect prey not only by killing them, but also by causing them to alter their behavior, including patterns of habitat selection.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Thus, zebrafish larvae have a unique repertoire of bouts reserved for hunting and produce stereotyped and dynamic sequences using these bouts.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Forage that involves a predatory mode.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1231780</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;These patterns remained consistent in models that controlled for differences in the hunting mode of the predator (stalking, coursing, or intermediate) and for differences among ecosystems.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">hunting</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>predation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1966</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/4083155</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>hunt forage</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The most significant characteristic of hunting behavior in Badis badis is that catching and eating each prey produces steadily increasing parsimony of behavior in capturing the next, if this is done at once.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
        <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Predation is a pervasive and unforgiving selection pressure on prey populations. A large and diverse body of research has explored the impacts of predation on the life history, morphology, physiology and behaviour of prey on both ecological and evolutionary time scales. Predators can exert adverse effects on their prey either directly by killing them or indirectly by instilling ‘fear’ in them, which induces behavioural, physiological and neurobiological costs on individuals attempting to avoid predation.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1238167</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Predators can affect prey not only by killing them, but also by causing them to alter their behavior, including patterns of habitat selection.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1231780</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Thus, zebrafish larvae have a unique repertoire of bouts reserved for hunting and produce stereotyped and dynamic sequences using these bouts.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000359 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000359">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000358"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000288"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-21T08:37:40.517299Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Hunt in which a predator moves relative to its prey.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym xml:lang="en">capture</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>harvest</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>roving hunt</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...roving predators such as the bonefish (Albula) and the pompano (Trachinotus)...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Here, we use unsupervised methods to study behavioral sequences in zebrafish larvae. We generate a map of swim bouts, revealing that fish modulate their tail movements along a continuum. During prey capture, larvae produce stereotyped sequences using a subset of bouts from a broader behavioral repertoire...

Capturing prey is an innate and essential behavior for the survival of many animals.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000359"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Hunt in which a predator moves relative to its prey.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000359"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">capture</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000359"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>harvest</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1988</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1126/science.239.4847.1525</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...the lagomorph harvest...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000359"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>roving hunt</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/68ddb3b7-6285-4bbf-935d-65630354bdba/download</dc:source>
        <dc:source rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1984</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...a roving method of hunting...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000359"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...roving predators such as the bonefish (Albula) and the pompano (Trachinotus)...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://scholarspace.manoa.hawaii.edu/bitstreams/68ddb3b7-6285-4bbf-935d-65630354bdba/download</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000359"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Here, we use unsupervised methods to study behavioral sequences in zebrafish larvae. We generate a map of swim bouts, revealing that fish modulate their tail movements along a continuum. During prey capture, larvae produce stereotyped sequences using a subset of bouts from a broader behavioral repertoire...

Capturing prey is an innate and essential behavior for the survival of many animals.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000360 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000360">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000381"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_00010483"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-21T20:03:37.22132Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Homeostasis by which the organism takes physical substance from the environment and incorporates it into its own body, and the reverse – active removal from the body and deposition into the environment.

Consumption includes taking in oxygen, water, amino acids, fats, other sources of energy and minerals. Excretion includes urination, CO2 release, defecation, vomiting.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>physical exchange</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>subsistence</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym xml:lang="en">consuming</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>excreting</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>subsistence homeostasis</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...thus the alimentary canal respires, digests, and excretes in the larva of the dragon-fly and in the fish Cobites.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;For many homeostatic resources (like water and oxygen), the organism will die in the long run if a subsistence level of consumption is not maintained, and it is in this sense that the organism needs these resources.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Male lawn-leaving depends on several sensory inputs that reflect the animal balancing its reproductive drive with its need to consume food.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000360"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Homeostasis by which the organism takes physical substance from the environment and incorporates it into its own body, and the reverse – active removal from the body and deposition into the environment.

Consumption includes taking in oxygen, water, amino acids, fats, other sources of energy and minerals. Excretion includes urination, CO2 release, defecation, vomiting.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1177/10597123211040574</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Axiom>
