    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000364 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000364">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000363"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000590"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000689"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_activity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-22T11:30:02.923272Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>pump filter feeding</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Filter that depends on an oscillating buccopharyngeal pump to generate feeding currents.

Continuous suction feeders remain relatively stationary when feeding.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">continuous suction</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">continuous-suction filter</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Ammocoetes and tadpoles, are small benthic or midwater forms for which mucus entrapment and ciliary transport are essential components of their suspension-feeding mechanism. They all have gill slits with internal entrapment surfaces that are part of their gill system.

The second group, containing suspension-feeding birds, is made up of air breathers that are restricted to feeding in either shallow water or at the water&apos;s surface. They lack gill slits and have lamellae at the margins of their jaws that form the filters.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000364"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasBroadSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>pump filter feeding</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1993</dc:date>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9780226315737</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000364"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Filter that depends on an oscillating buccopharyngeal pump to generate feeding currents.

Continuous suction feeders remain relatively stationary when feeding.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>ISBN:9780226315737</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Continuous Suction Feeders. An odd collection of animals comes under this heading: ammocoetes, tadpoles, certain ducks, and flamingos (table 2.4). What these organisms share is a dependence on an oscillating buccopharyngeal pump to generate feeding currents. Continuous suction feeders remain relatively stationary when feeding and thus, unlike most other suspension feeders, their locomotor capability is not directly linked to their feeding morphology.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000364"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">continuous suction</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1993</dc:date>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9780226315737</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000364"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">continuous-suction filter</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000364"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Ammocoetes and tadpoles, are small benthic or midwater forms for which mucus entrapment and ciliary transport are essential components of their suspension-feeding mechanism. They all have gill slits with internal entrapment surfaces that are part of their gill system.

The second group, containing suspension-feeding birds, is made up of air breathers that are restricted to feeding in either shallow water or at the water&apos;s surface. They lack gill slits and have lamellae at the margins of their jaws that form the filters.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9780226315737</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000365 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000365">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000363"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000122"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000427"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_activity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-22T11:40:03.385211Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Filter that engulfs a volume of water by swimming forward with an open mouth, and fill the buccal cavity in a single pulse.

In order for intermittent ram feeding to work, the inertial forces must be very high and the buccal floor compliant, otherwise the pressure head anterior to the animal would push water and food away.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">intermittent ram</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">intermittent-ram filter</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;The rorqual whales direct their attacks on schools of fishes or local concentrations of krill. Complex herding behaviors may be used to further concentrate prey in front of the whales before they open their mouths.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000365"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Filter that engulfs a volume of water by swimming forward with an open mouth, and fill the buccal cavity in a single pulse.

In order for intermittent ram feeding to work, the inertial forces must be very high and the buccal floor compliant, otherwise the pressure head anterior to the animal would push water and food away.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>ISBN:9780226315737</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Ram feeders engulf a volume of water by swimming forward with an open mouth (figs. 2.1, 2.2)... Intermittent ram feeders use their forward velocity to force their mouths open widely (fig. 2.3) and fill their buccal cavity in a single pulse... In order for intermittent ram feeding to work, the inertial forces must be very high and the buccal floor compliant, otherwise the pressure head anterior to the animal would push water and food away.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000365"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">intermittent ram</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1993</dc:date>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9780226315737</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000365"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">intermittent-ram filter</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000365"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The rorqual whales direct their attacks on schools of fishes or local concentrations of krill. Complex herding behaviors may be used to further concentrate prey in front of the whales before they open their mouths.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9780226315737</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000366 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000366">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000363"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000590"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000427"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_activity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-22T11:45:12.871656Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>pump filter feeding</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Filter that generates aperiodic pulses of water into the mouth, but does not usually alter its swimming speed or direction to focus attention on individual plankters.

A predator with a gape that is large enough to engulf more than one prey at a time may use intermittent suction feeding when the prey are not dense enough or are too large to elicit continuous ram feeding.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>gulping</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">intermittent suction</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">intermittent-suction filter</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000366"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasBroadSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>pump filter feeding</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1993</dc:date>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9780226315737</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000366"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Filter that generates aperiodic pulses of water into the mouth, but does not usually alter its swimming speed or direction to focus attention on individual plankters.

A predator with a gape that is large enough to engulf more than one prey at a time may use intermittent suction feeding when the prey are not dense enough or are too large to elicit continuous ram feeding.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>ISBN:9780226315737</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Compared to continuous suction feeders, species in this category generate suction with aperiodic pulses... The way water is transported into the mouth by intermittent suction feeders is similar to that used by the myriad of planktivorous fishes that visually locate, attack, and engulf individual prey items using suction. However, during suspension feeding, the predator does not usually alter its swimming speed or direction to focus attention on individual plankters.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000366"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>gulping</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1993</dc:date>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9780226315737</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000366"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">intermittent suction</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1993</dc:date>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9780226315737</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000366"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">intermittent-suction filter</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000341"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MF_0000003"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_mental_process"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-22T20:33:29.855664Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Maintenance in which an immediate behavioral response is modulated by the sympathetic nervous system, which temporarily suspends normal life functions.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>acute stress</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>alarm</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>fear-like</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>fear</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>fright</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>anxiety</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>anxiety-like</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>alarm maintenance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000382"/>
        <skos:closeMatch>NBO:0000470</skos:closeMatch>
        <skos:example>&quot;Alarm reaction may also include freezing with frequent opercular movements, changes in shoaling (e.g., rapid 1–2 s decreases of shoal cohesion followed by longer-lasting, up to several minutes, increase of shoal cohesion) and diving.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Ardeola ibis...
Birds will often respond to Alert in one of their numbers by following suit, so that the attitude acts as an alarm signal.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Maintenance in which an immediate behavioral response is modulated by the sympathetic nervous system, which temporarily suspends normal life functions.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1181774</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Acute stressors are short-term, such as a storm, or an encounter with a predator or aggressive conspecific, resulting in immediate physiological or behavioral responses modulated by the sympathetic nervous system, which temporarily suspend normal life functions.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>acute stress</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2023</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1181774</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>alarm</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...animals not being much alarmed by danger...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>fear-like</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2013</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1089/zeb.2012.0861</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>fear</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>fright</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>anxiety</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>anxiety-like</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2020</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1002/jnr.24550</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>alarm maintenance</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Alarm reaction may also include freezing with frequent opercular movements, changes in shoaling (e.g., rapid 1–2 s decreases of shoal cohesion followed by longer-lasting, up to several minutes, increase of shoal cohesion) and diving.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1089/zeb.2012.0861</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Ardeola ibis...
Birds will often respond to Alert in one of their numbers by following suit, so that the attitude acts as an alarm signal.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000368 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000368">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000178"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001769"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-05T11:34:06.006575Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Travel that is oriented along a perceived environmental gradient.

This may have positive or negative valence.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">taxis</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">taxis travel</rdfs:label>
        <skos:closeMatch>NBO:0000375</skos:closeMatch>
        <skos:example>&quot;Short integration windows for deciding the next behavior have been observed in thermal navigation of larvae and social affiliation of juvenile zebrafish.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The most extreme type of Avoiding is panicky flight ; the behavior has negative valence since the only taxis is away from the stimulus, if the stimulus is located.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;This is necessary in order to distinguish the invariable fixed motor pattern of the activity from the variable orientating element, the taxis.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000368"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Travel that is oriented along a perceived environmental gradient.

This may have positive or negative valence.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1038/s41467-023-38685-1</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000368"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">taxis</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000368"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">taxis travel</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000368"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Short integration windows for deciding the next behavior have been observed in thermal navigation of larvae and social affiliation of juvenile zebrafish.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2019.11.022</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000368"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The most extreme type of Avoiding is panicky flight ; the behavior has negative valence since the only taxis is away from the stimulus, if the stimulus is located.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000368"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;This is necessary in order to distinguish the invariable fixed motor pattern of the activity from the variable orientating element, the taxis.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000369 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000369">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0010000"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-22T20:48:53.274472Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Alarm which involves the self-controlled loss of a body part at a predetermined breakage location.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">autotomy</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>autotomy alarm</rdfs:label>
        <skos:broadMatch>NBO:0070000</skos:broadMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...many arthropods have limbs that may be autotomized. The animals frequently regenerate such appendages, but in the meantime, they must adjust their locomotion to compensate for imbalances created by the loss of such limbs.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;To differentiate autotomy from moulting/shedding, we also specify that autotomy should not be restricted to a certain climatic season or transitional period during development. We find this purely descriptive definition to be suitable for numerous reasons. First, autotomy has additional benefits beyond that of escaping predation, thus whether an individual uses autotomy in an anti-predatory context should not be incorporated into the definition. Second, by removing adaptive benefits from the definition, we can also begin to move away from a purely adaptive perspective and therefore broaden our scope, and hopefully our understanding, of autotomy.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000369"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Alarm which involves the self-controlled loss of a body part at a predetermined breakage location.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1111/brv.12539</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Herein, we simply define autotomy as the self-controlled loss of a body part at a predetermined breakage location.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000369"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">autotomy</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1986</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.120.1.283</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000369"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>autotomy alarm</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000369"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...many arthropods have limbs that may be autotomized. The animals frequently regenerate such appendages, but in the meantime, they must adjust their locomotion to compensate for imbalances created by the loss of such limbs.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.120.1.283</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000369"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;To differentiate autotomy from moulting/shedding, we also specify that autotomy should not be restricted to a certain climatic season or transitional period during development. We find this purely descriptive definition to be suitable for numerous reasons. First, autotomy has additional benefits beyond that of escaping predation, thus whether an individual uses autotomy in an anti-predatory context should not be incorporated into the definition. Second, by removing adaptive benefits from the definition, we can also begin to move away from a purely adaptive perspective and therefore broaden our scope, and hopefully our understanding, of autotomy.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/brv.12539</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000370 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000370">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000369"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000931"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_activity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-22T20:49:26.032133Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Autotomy that is behaviorally controlled.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">true autotomy</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000370"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Autotomy that is behaviorally controlled.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Morphological adaptations include cryptic as well as brilliant coloration and specialized structures such as the skeletal, vascular, and muscular arrangement found in lizards that have true tail autotomy - behaviorally controlled tail-loss.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000370"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">true autotomy</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2002</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...true tail autotomy...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000371 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000371">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001638"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-22T20:49:45.086337Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Alarm that responds in place to an overly close approach.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>defence</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">defense</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>defence alarm</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Positive defense consists of spreading the fins. This may be accompanied by rotating the body slightly, presenting the erect dorsal fin spines to the object eliciting the behavior.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The ostrich indeed inhabits continents and is exposed to danger from which it cannot escape by flight, but by kicking it can defend itself from enemies, as well as any of the smaller quadrupeds.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:relatedMatch>NBO:0000636</skos:relatedMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Defensive behavior can also be used to indicate the level of acute stress and can be readily measured in rattlesnakes, which are known for their elaborate defensive displays that include head-elevated coiling, tailrattling, striking, and venomous bites.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000371"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Alarm that responds in place to an overly close approach.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Howver. many animals that defend a territory live in colonies (gulls, terns, herons, the lizard Anolis sagrei, Centrarchid fish etc); they apparently seek each other&apos;s company when establishing a nesting place, but they do not like their fellows to approach them beyond certain limits.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000371"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>defence</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000371"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">defense</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1957</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&amp;context=wilson_bulletin</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000371"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>defence alarm</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000371"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Positive defense consists of spreading the fins. This may be accompanied by rotating the body slightly, presenting the erect dorsal fin spines to the object eliciting the behavior.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000371"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The ostrich indeed inhabits continents and is exposed to danger from which it cannot escape by flight, but by kicking it can defend itself from enemies, as well as any of the smaller quadrupeds.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000371"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Defensive behavior can also be used to indicate the level of acute stress and can be readily measured in rattlesnakes, which are known for their elaborate defensive displays that include head-elevated coiling, tailrattling, striking, and venomous bites.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1181774</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000372 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000372">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000371"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000385"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-22T20:50:06.918618Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Defence that is aggressive.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>active defence</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>active defense</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>aggressive defence</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>aggressive defense</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">aggressively defending</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>resistance</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>resistance defence</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;In scorpions, it is speculated that the injection of a pre-venom into the female’s body tranquilises females and so reduces physical resistance to mating.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Nocomis also exhibits extreme sexual dimorphism during the breeding season. Nuptial males develop sharp cephalic tubercles and aggressively defend their spawning sites from conspecific intruders and potential egg predators such as suckers.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:narrowMatch>NBO:0020230</skos:narrowMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Antipredator behavioural responses to perceived acute predation threats (i.e. fear of predation) include increased vigilance, grouping behaviour, avoidance (through camouflage, reduced activity, seeking refuge or fleeing, for example), deterrence behaviour and aggressive defence.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000372"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Defence that is aggressive.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1238167</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Antipredator behavioural responses to perceived acute predation threats (i.e. fear of predation) include increased vigilance, grouping behaviour, avoidance (through camouflage, reduced activity, seeking refuge or fleeing, for example), deterrence behaviour and aggressive defence.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000372"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>active defence</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2015</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1098/rsbl.2015.0325</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000372"/>
