    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000378 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000378">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000367"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MF_0000060"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_mental_process"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-10-22T20:52:37.920668Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Alarm that agonistically defends a resource.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>guarding</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">protection</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>resource defence</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>resource defense</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>resource monopolization</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>on the watch</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">monopolization alarm</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000293"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;...even the tiger in India most rarely dares to attack a young elephant protected by its dam.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;It is not known whether this high percentage of attentiveness occurs in other Ardeidae, but its function in A. ibis is almost certainly to_protect the eggs and young from predators and the nest itself from stick-stealing birds.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Where males defend territories, receptive females commonly show a strong preference both for joining territorial males rather than nonterritorial males and for joining males that are already defending large harems rather than those without females or with small harems. Some of the clearest evidence here comes from studies of lek-breeding ungulates such as fallow deer, Uganda kob and Kafue lechwe which live in large unstable herds of mixed membership, lacking a consistent male dominance hierarchy.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000378"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Alarm that agonistically defends a resource.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1002/(SICI)1098-2337(1998)24:3&lt;197::AID-AB4&gt;3.0.CO;2-I</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
