    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000384"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000553"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_realization"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-05T11:38:21.811703Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Agonism that involves rivalry.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">competition</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>contest</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>competition agonism</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Bats and common nighthawks (Chordeiles minor) have been observed in competitive interaction over insects at a human light source.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...changes in habitat use to avoid predationmay be constrained by competition: larger, dominant competitors should respond freely to predation risk, but the responses of smaller, subordinate competitors may be constrained by the responses of dominant competitors.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Many factors other than predation risk affect habitat selection. Notably, population density alters habitat selection within a species or foraging guild. At low density, all individuals can occupy high quality patches with little competition, but as competitors accumulate in high quality habitat patches, changing cost-benefit ratios favor the use of lower quality patches, particularly by subordinate competitors.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Thalassoma cupido...
Yet under favorable conditions, initial phase males will also compete for access to single females.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The potential fitness benefits to an individual expressing one or more of these antipredator behaviours is increased survivorship and reproductive success, but at the potential fitness-related costs of expended energy and lost behavioural opportunities such as foraging, mating, parental care and territorial defence. Vice versa, engaging in any of the latter competing behavioural activities may increase an individual’s susceptibility to predation.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Agonism that involves rivalry.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Amongst birds, the contest is often of a more peaceful character. All those who have attended to the subject, believe that there is the severest rivalry between the males of many species to attract by singing the females.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">competition</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>contest</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>competition agonism</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Bats and common nighthawks (Chordeiles minor) have been observed in competitive interaction over insects at a human light source.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/978-3-319-68576-2_3</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...changes in habitat use to avoid predationmay be constrained by competition: larger, dominant competitors should respond freely to predation risk, but the responses of smaller, subordinate competitors may be constrained by the responses of dominant competitors.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1231780</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Many factors other than predation risk affect habitat selection. Notably, population density alters habitat selection within a species or foraging guild. At low density, all individuals can occupy high quality patches with little competition, but as competitors accumulate in high quality habitat patches, changing cost-benefit ratios favor the use of lower quality patches, particularly by subordinate competitors.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1231780</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Thalassoma cupido...
Yet under favorable conditions, initial phase males will also compete for access to single females.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/jji1950/24/2/24_2_101/_article/-char/ja/</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The potential fitness benefits to an individual expressing one or more of these antipredator behaviours is increased survivorship and reproductive success, but at the potential fitness-related costs of expended energy and lost behavioural opportunities such as foraging, mating, parental care and territorial defence. Vice versa, engaging in any of the latter competing behavioural activities may increase an individual’s susceptibility to predation.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1238167</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


