        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/gait"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-07-20T16:21:03.25417Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Creeping gait in which a fish alternately releases and adheres its anterior sucker, in conjunction with the inward and outward swing of its caudal region respectively.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>suctorial movement</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">sucker gait</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...in the waterfall climbing gobies... the oral sucker and pelvic fins drive waterfall climbing...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Astroblepus longifilis inhabiting mountain torrents is able to hold tightly fast to smooth surfaces by use of its suctorial mouth, both under water and out of it.. The method of progressing overland as seen by the author is essentially a fish-like flapping coupled with an appropriate action of the mouth, it holding fast on the flexure of the caudal portion, and releasing on the extension.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000401"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Creeping gait in which a fish alternately releases and adheres its anterior sucker, in conjunction with the inward and outward swing of its caudal region respectively.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000401"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>suctorial movement</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...suctorial disc ... movements of these discs...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000401"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">sucker gait</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000401"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...in the waterfall climbing gobies... the oral sucker and pelvic fins drive waterfall climbing...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icac061</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000401"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Astroblepus longifilis inhabiting mountain torrents is able to hold tightly fast to smooth surfaces by use of its suctorial mouth, both under water and out of it.. The method of progressing overland as seen by the author is essentially a fish-like flapping coupled with an appropriate action of the mouth, it holding fast on the flexure of the caudal portion, and releasing on the extension.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000308"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001019"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-11T09:18:09.469196Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>inhabiting</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Residence in which an animal inhabits a constrained space, depending on habitat limitations and density-regulating behaviour.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>occupancy</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">occupation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">occupancy residence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000415"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;Snail shells are in short supply and hermit crabs compete for this limited resource. The crabs often occupy shells that appear less than optimum in shape or size.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Many of the more generalizable definitions of habitat offer some reference to occupancy or  resources that promote occupancy. Of these, Morrison et al.’s is among the more precise and generalizable, calling habitat an “area with a combination of resources and environmental conditions that promotes occupancy by individuals of a given species that allows individuals to survive and reproduce.”&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The number of B. amplus associated with each colony of J. juncea and D. gemmacea, was termed occupation density.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasBroadSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>inhabiting</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2009</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://www.science.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/sites/11/2024/05/s22rbz145-157.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Residence in which an animal inhabits a constrained space, depending on habitat limitations and density-regulating behaviour.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>https://www.science.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/sites/11/2024/05/s22rbz145-157.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>occupancy</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2000</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/j.1744-7429.2000.tb00506.x</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">occupation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...pre-occupation has probably played an important part in checking the commingling of species...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">occupancy residence</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Snail shells are in short supply and hermit crabs compete for this limited resource. The crabs often occupy shells that appear less than optimum in shape or size.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.120.1.283</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Many of the more generalizable definitions of habitat offer some reference to occupancy or  resources that promote occupancy. Of these, Morrison et al.’s is among the more precise and generalizable, calling habitat an “area with a combination of resources and environmental conditions that promotes occupancy by individuals of a given species that allows individuals to survive and reproduce.”&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/978-3-319-68576-2_3</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The number of B. amplus associated with each colony of J. juncea and D. gemmacea, was termed occupation density.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://www.science.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/sites/11/2024/05/s22rbz145-157.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000403 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000403">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000086"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000003"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000173"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000182"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000183"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/locomotion_realizing_regime"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-07-21T08:06:51.704708Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>aerial excursion</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Interfacing-regime that involves a rapid swimming up through the surface of the water, momentum alone carrying the animal forward after it has left the water entirely.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>emersing percent</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>leaping</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">emersion regime</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;If the body is held rigidly in a straight line the path will be straight, barring external factors. Mugil usually leaps in this manner. If the body is flexed, the fish follows the curve and falls to the concave side. Salmo and Tarpon usually show this. The final stroke before leaving the water is often of great amplitude in these fishes and sends them upward in a great curving path.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000403"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasBroadSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>aerial excursion</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000403"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Interfacing-regime that involves a rapid swimming up through the surface of the water, momentum alone carrying the animal forward after it has left the water entirely.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.5962/p.203769</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The leaping of fishes may be considered simply as a rapid swimming up through the surface of the water, momentum alone carrying the fish forward after the tail has left the water entirely.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000403"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>emersing percent</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1995</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.198.1.79</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...emersing up to 7.2 % of volume...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000403"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>leaping</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000403"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">emersion regime</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000403"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;If the body is held rigidly in a straight line the path will be straight, barring external factors. Mugil usually leaps in this manner. If the body is flexed, the fish follows the curve and falls to the concave side. Salmo and Tarpon usually show this. The final stroke before leaving the water is often of great amplitude in these fishes and sends them upward in a great curving path.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000404 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000404">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000463"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000023"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_realization"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-11T09:20:09.341529Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Cooperation in which charge of a particular, mutually beneficial, behaviour is assumed.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>on duty</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">role</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>role cooperation</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Until the beginning of the spawning it is the male that usually behaves more aggressively, but as soon as the female has laid some eggs. the roles change, particularly in the centre of the territory. Thereafter the female often drives away the male with butting and tail-beating. The male is then chiefly in charge of defending the periphery of the territory. We observed this especially frequently in C. meeki.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;More work is needed that integrates assessments of social contextual factors like density and variation in social roles with assessments of individual behavior to increase our understanding of behavioral variation across populations and across generations.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000404"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Cooperation in which charge of a particular, mutually beneficial, behaviour is assumed.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000404"/>
