    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000412">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000036"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000183"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_realization"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-11T17:17:18.033197Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Swarm that is aquatic.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">shoal</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>schooling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>troop</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>shoal swarm</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000413"/>
        <skos:closeMatch>NBO:0040001</skos:closeMatch>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000412"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Swarm that is aquatic.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1089/zeb.2012.0861</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000412"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">shoal</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1973</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/BF01609520</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000412"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>schooling</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>The 1950 source does not mention shoaling throughout, but it was typical not to differentiate in ethological literature at the time. It notes, &quot;It is very typical of these schools that the members continually show a tendency to practice the same actions simultaneously.&quot; However, these actions do not relate to polarity, but to foraging, floating, or lying on the bottom. Additionally, attention is drawn at one point to a school of fry protected by parents in which, &quot;the young may move in different directions. But the school as a whole remains near or round the leading fish...&quot; And elsewhere, &quot;As long as the female swims quietly, the young, mostly feeding, are swimming criss-cross within the school.&quot; This seems very like the definition of shoaling. Most likely the term &apos;school&apos; in this source predominantly relates to the modern &apos;shoal&apos;, but without more certainty, this synonym is being applied to the last mutual ancestor of both terms.</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000412"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>troop</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000412"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>shoal swarm</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000413 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000413">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000411"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000183"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000406"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000662"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/group_behaviour"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-11T17:17:59.425196Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Aggregation that is 3-dimensional, polarized, maintained and aquatic.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">schooling</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>school aggregation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000412"/>
        <skos:closeMatch>NBO:0040002</skos:closeMatch>
        <skos:example>&quot;In ecological systems, examples of synchrony include flashing of fireflies for courting behavior, schooling behavior in fishes for predator avoidance and reproduction, flocking of birds, and spawning of gametes by corals for sexual reproduction.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;As each vortex is shed by individual swimming fish... a fish situated laterally midway between the two fish of the preceding column... avoids having to overcome increased incoming flow... The advantage is greater when fish in the same column swim in antiphase with the neighbors. These requirements point to an elongated diamond-shape pattern as the basic optimum structure in fish schools... Magnuson estimates average energy savings of 10%–20% from schooling.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;School (schooling): Formation of a relatively polarized group (school) in which multiple fish swim together, in a coordinated/synchronous fashion;&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000413"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Aggregation that is 3-dimensional, polarized, maintained and aquatic.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/BF01609520</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000413"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">schooling</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1973</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/BF01609520</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000413"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>school aggregation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000413"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In ecological systems, examples of synchrony include flashing of fireflies for courting behavior, schooling behavior in fishes for predator avoidance and reproduction, flocking of birds, and spawning of gametes by corals for sexual reproduction.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/bc60659126ef5c0172c2392d485b0c9b/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000413"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;As each vortex is shed by individual swimming fish... a fish situated laterally midway between the two fish of the preceding column... avoids having to overcome increased incoming flow... The advantage is greater when fish in the same column swim in antiphase with the neighbors. These requirements point to an elongated diamond-shape pattern as the basic optimum structure in fish schools... Magnuson estimates average energy savings of 10%–20% from schooling.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1109/48.757275</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000413"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;School (schooling): Formation of a relatively polarized group (school) in which multiple fish swim together, in a coordinated/synchronous fashion;&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1089/zeb.2012.0861</dc:source>
