    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000383"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="genid3126"/>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_realization"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-12T11:26:34.724781Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>communication</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Interaction that serves a communicative function.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>animal language</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">signal</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>advertisement</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>display</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>signal interaction</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Alongside territorial signals, which function to keep unwanted intruders at bay, some architecture may function as an attractive signal, by conveying information to potential mates about the quality of the builder. Bowerbirds provide one of the most extravagant examples of built structures functioning as such advertisements. Male bowerbirds construct bowers from interwoven sticks and decorate the fl oor of their courtship arena with all manner of colorful objects, each assembled into discrete piles with great precision, including berries, shells, and even human-made objects, like bottle caps or poker chips.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:relatedMatch>NBO:0000612</skos:relatedMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Communication is therefore largely about changes induced in an individual by a perceived action or structure of another individual. It is thus the social behavior that lies at the foundation of all other social behavior and includes individuals producing visual displays that deter the pursuit of a predator, vocalizations that change the movement patterns of others in their midst, volatile chemical compounds that attract potential mates, and vibrational waves that pause the signaling behavior of nearby conspecifics.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Sometimes a noisy Wing-flap occurs instead of the Shake. There is much variation in this display; sometimes the belly feathers are nibbled vigorously or the bill may not touch the body at all merely swinging briefly across above the feet.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Restriction rdf:nodeID="genid3126">
        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000397"/>
    </owl:Restriction>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid3126"/>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...movements in secondary situations which differ in form from their &quot;normal&quot; counterparts are considered to have undergone a process of &quot;ritualization&quot;; they are referred to as &quot;displays&quot;...&quot;

doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasBroadSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>communication</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Interaction that serves a communicative function.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.3389/fetho.2022.1066186</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Unlike cues, signals have evolved for the particular communicative function they serve.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>animal language</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">signal</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414"/>
