        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Alongside territorial signals, which function to keep unwanted intruders at bay, some architecture may function as an attractive signal, by conveying information to potential mates about the quality of the builder. Bowerbirds provide one of the most extravagant examples of built structures functioning as such advertisements. Male bowerbirds construct bowers from interwoven sticks and decorate the fl oor of their courtship arena with all manner of colorful objects, each assembled into discrete piles with great precision, including berries, shells, and even human-made objects, like bottle caps or poker chips.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2021.09.082</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Communication is therefore largely about changes induced in an individual by a perceived action or structure of another individual. It is thus the social behavior that lies at the foundation of all other social behavior and includes individuals producing visual displays that deter the pursuit of a predator, vocalizations that change the movement patterns of others in their midst, volatile chemical compounds that attract potential mates, and vibrational waves that pause the signaling behavior of nearby conspecifics.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3389/fetho.2022.1066186</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000414"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Sometimes a noisy Wing-flap occurs instead of the Shake. There is much variation in this display; sometimes the belly feathers are nibbled vigorously or the bill may not touch the body at all merely swinging briefly across above the feet.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
