    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000415 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000415">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000773"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000069"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_life_cycle"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-11T17:19:41.32525Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Development in which a larva settles into a preferred habitat, transitioning into a juvenile.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">recruitment</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>settlement</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">recruitment development</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000402"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;...the multiple-branching form of D. gemmacea colonies and their orientation of these branches into the current leads to an increased exposure to drifting plankton, which may in turn increase the chances of fish larvae encountering and eventually settling onto D. gemmacea compared to J. juncea.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Conversely, in contrast to investigating control of synchrony, it would be interesting to investigate the effects of variation in spawning synchrony on recruitment for studies related to population connectivity. This work would involve  determining recruitment as a function of three components: 1) the reproductive effort of an individual and the densities of individuals in a given area; 2) the exact time at which spawning occurs; and 3) larval transport after spawning. Hughes and  colleagues reported that colony fecundity explained 49-72% of the variance in larval recruitment of various coral species.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000415"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Development in which a larva settles into a preferred habitat, transitioning into a juvenile.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>https://www.science.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/sites/11/2024/05/s22rbz145-157.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000415"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">recruitment</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2000</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/S0169-5347(99)01744-9</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;recruitment-limited&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000415"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>settlement</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2000</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/S0169-5347(99)01744-9</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;post-settlement&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000415"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">recruitment development</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000415"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...the multiple-branching form of D. gemmacea colonies and their orientation of these branches into the current leads to an increased exposure to drifting plankton, which may in turn increase the chances of fish larvae encountering and eventually settling onto D. gemmacea compared to J. juncea.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://www.science.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/sites/11/2024/05/s22rbz145-157.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000415"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Conversely, in contrast to investigating control of synchrony, it would be interesting to investigate the effects of variation in spawning synchrony on recruitment for studies related to population connectivity. This work would involve  determining recruitment as a function of three components: 1) the reproductive effort of an individual and the densities of individuals in a given area; 2) the exact time at which spawning occurs; and 3) larval transport after spawning. Hughes and  colleagues reported that colony fecundity explained 49-72% of the variance in larval recruitment of various coral species.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/bc60659126ef5c0172c2392d485b0c9b/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000416 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000416">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000117"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000463"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-12T11:28:02.671656Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym xml:lang="en">chemical communication</oboInOwl:hasBroadSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Modality that is transmitted via a substance.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>chemical signal</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>chemical stimulus</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>semiochemical communication</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>pheromone</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>semiochemical modality</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;It has been shown in Lebistes reticulatus (Peters) and in Rhodeus amarus Boch that the males produces hormones which, when absorbed by the females via the surrounding water, promote the ripening of the ovaries.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Given the prevalence of noise in most every channel of communication – and the increasing presence of human noise in non-human animal communication – more research on signal and cue perception in the contexts of acoustic, chemical, visual noise is needed.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;There is also evidence that alarm pheromones found in the discharge might serve to warn conspecifics of a predator threat, parallel to the shreckstoff found in fishes and chemical communication of stress found in larval salamanders and tadpoles.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000416"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasBroadSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">chemical communication</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2002</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000416"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Modality that is transmitted via a substance.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000416"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
