Whenever the fish is threatening, seven black vertical bands appear...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000457"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Red sea bream Pagrus major and crimson sea bream, Evynnis japonica...

The territory holder threatens the invaders by slightly dipping its head.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/j.1444-2906.2003.00797.x</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000457"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Tools can be used to create and mediate influence interactions: a brandished stick might communicate a more effective threat than a bare fist, for example...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.anbehav.2007.09.028</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000458 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000458">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000464"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000055"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000001"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_realization"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-17T21:05:10.599718Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Manipulation that does not reveal a truthful correspondence with a state of motivation.

The evolution of checks on deception requires some time, so that over the long course of evolution there should be oscillations between deception and truthfulness. Small deceptions should persist longest. Ironically, the cleverest deceptions are in the long run the most ineffective, because they most quickly become common among competitors.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">deception</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>poker face</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>deception manipulation</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Some deceptions, e.g., mimicry of females by subordinant male sticklebacks who may thereby be able to sneak some eggs or fertilizations...&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000458"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Manipulation that does not reveal a truthful correspondence with a state of motivation.

The evolution of checks on deception requires some time, so that over the long course of evolution there should be oscillations between deception and truthfulness. Small deceptions should persist longest. Ironically, the cleverest deceptions are in the long run the most ineffective, because they most quickly become common among competitors.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000458"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">deception</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1979</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000458"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>poker face</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1979</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000458"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>deception manipulation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000458"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Some deceptions, e.g., mimicry of females by subordinant male sticklebacks who may thereby be able to sneak some eggs or fertilizations...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0001010"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000441"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-17T21:05:35.008038Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Mimicry which depicts an animal part that enables a predator to attract its prey to within striking range.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>Peckhamian mimicry</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">luring</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>For now we are including lures, but...</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">aggressive mimicry</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...the slender lure from tissue inside the lower jaw of some of the stargazers (Uranoscopus) is enticingly wriggled like a worm from the mouth of an otherwise hidden stargazer.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Perhaps the best-known examples of aggressive mimicry are found in the frogfish family Antennariidae (and other ceratioids) that use a modified 1st dorsal ray as a lure called the illicium.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The only other case involves one species of assassin bug (S. variegata), which holds termite corpses as lures for other termites); however, recent research has revealed that the feather-legged assassin bug (Ptilocnemus lemur) also uses a lure, though in this case it uses its own leg.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;A more recent suggestion is that the incipient rattle evolved as an improvement for the caudal luring of prey, a behavior exhibited by many pitvipers and a few colubrids.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Mimicry which depicts an animal part that enables a predator to attract its prey to within striking range.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>https://zoolstud.sinica.edu.tw/Journals/44.3/299.pdf</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Aggressive mimicry, sometimes known as Peckhamian mimicry (after E.G. Peckham, who discovered it while studying spiders), is used to depict an animal or a part thereof that enables a predator to get closer to its prey or to attract its prey to within striking range.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Peckhamian mimicry</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2005</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://zoolstud.sinica.edu.tw/Journals/44.3/299.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">luring</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2002</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>For now we are including lures, but...</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>https://zoolstud.sinica.edu.tw/Journals/44.3/299.pdf</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Sazima... preferred not to regard lures from part of the body of predatory fishes as true examples of aggressive mimicry.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">aggressive mimicry</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2002</dc:date>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9789798105975</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...the slender lure from tissue inside the lower jaw of some of the stargazers (Uranoscopus) is enticingly wriggled like a worm from the mouth of an otherwise hidden stargazer.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://zoolstud.sinica.edu.tw/Journals/44.3/299.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Perhaps the best-known examples of aggressive mimicry are found in the frogfish family Antennariidae (and other ceratioids) that use a modified 1st dorsal ray as a lure called the illicium.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://zoolstud.sinica.edu.tw/Journals/44.3/299.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The only other case involves one species of assassin bug (S. variegata), which holds termite corpses as lures for other termites); however, recent research has revealed that the feather-legged assassin bug (Ptilocnemus lemur) also uses a lure, though in this case it uses its own leg.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000459"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;A more recent suggestion is that the incipient rattle evolved as an improvement for the caudal luring of prey, a behavior exhibited by many pitvipers and a few colubrids.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/tops.12554</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000458"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000504"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-17T21:06:06.146705Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Deception that is characterised by immobility with a prone but stiff posture maintained by pronounced tonic muscular activity; limbs, which if moved by an external force maintain the newly imposed position for long periods; and unresponsiveness to external stimuli, while remaining fully aware of the environment.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>animal hypnosis</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">death feigning</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>playing possum</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>thanatosis</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>tonic immobility</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>catatonia</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>thanatosis deception</rdfs:label>
        <skos:closeMatch>NBO:0020199</skos:closeMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Apomorphic characters were the derived body throwing and tail hiding behavior of H. torquata, R. lecontei, and L. getula, the elevated tail coiling and wriggling of T. marcianus, and the ventral exposure of the head and body by D. punctatus (in death feigning behavior).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Specialized and stereotyped antipredator behavior in snakes includes death feigning, envenomation, cloacal popping, cloacal sac discharge (musk), coiled tail displays, S-coil defensive postures, body balling and other tightly coiled defensive postures, tail vibration, mouth hissing, and dermal sound production.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Deception that is characterised by immobility with a prone but stiff posture maintained by pronounced tonic muscular activity; limbs, which if moved by an external force maintain the newly imposed position for long periods; and unresponsiveness to external stimuli, while remaining fully aware of the environment.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2014.08.051</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Thanatosis is characterised by a number of different features: catalepsy, immobility with a prone but stiff posture maintained by pronounced tonic muscular activity; ‘waxy flexibility’ of the limbs, which if moved by an external force maintain the newly imposed position for long periods; and unresponsiveness to external stimuli, while remaining fully aware of the environment.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>animal hypnosis</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2014</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2014.08.051</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">death feigning</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2002</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>playing possum</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2014</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2014.08.051</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>thanatosis</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2014</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2014.08.051</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>tonic immobility</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2014</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2014.08.051</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>catatonia</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2014</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2014.08.051</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>thanatosis deception</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Apomorphic characters were the derived body throwing and tail hiding behavior of H. torquata, R. lecontei, and L. getula, the elevated tail coiling and wriggling of T. marcianus, and the ventral exposure of the head and body by D. punctatus (in death feigning behavior).&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000460"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Specialized and stereotyped antipredator behavior in snakes includes death feigning, envenomation, cloacal popping, cloacal sac discharge (musk), coiled tail displays, S-coil defensive postures, body balling and other tightly coiled defensive postures, tail vibration, mouth hissing, and dermal sound production.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000461 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000461">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000458"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000464"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-17T21:06:41.398143Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Deception that avoids detection.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">crypsis</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>crypsis deception</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;These findings constitute the first study of reptile social buffering and add additional support to the growing evidence that rattlesnakes are cryptically social.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:narrowMatch>NBO:0020139</skos:narrowMatch>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000461"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Deception that avoids detection.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>https://search.proquest.com/openview/163eedf011944cd2321739b51d33470d/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Lastly, I call attention to the fact that all of these snakes rely first on crypsis, the avoidance of detection by predators as an antipredator strategy.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000461"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">crypsis</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1999</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.202.6.661</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000461"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>crypsis deception</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000461"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;These findings constitute the first study of reptile social buffering and add additional support to the growing evidence that rattlesnakes are cryptically social.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3389/fetho.2023.1181774</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000462 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000462">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0001009"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000513"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_activity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-17T21:06:55.936712Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Resemblance in which changed body colouration matches changed background lighting.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>background adaptation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">camouflaging</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>camouflaging resemblance</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Tilapia natalensis...
Vertical bands mostly appear when the fishes no longer feel at ease, for instance the appearance of individuals of other species. it is striking that just in those circumstances in which it might become necessary for the fish to hide itself, the vertical bands are developed, which undoubtedly make the fish less visible between plants because of their camouflaging effect.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;We immediately think of flatfishes that match the surrounding substratum and quickly change their pattern as they move over a different colored area...&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000462"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Resemblance in which changed body colouration matches changed background lighting.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1089/zeb.2012.0861</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000462"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>background adaptation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2013</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1089/zeb.2012.0861</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000462"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">camouflaging</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000462"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>camouflaging resemblance</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000462"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Tilapia natalensis...
Vertical bands mostly appear when the fishes no longer feel at ease, for instance the appearance of individuals of other species. it is striking that just in those circumstances in which it might become necessary for the fish to hide itself, the vertical bands are developed, which undoubtedly make the fish less visible between plants because of their camouflaging effect.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000462"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;We immediately think of flatfishes that match the surrounding substratum and quickly change their pattern as they move over a different colored area...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://zoolstud.sinica.edu.tw/Journals/44.3/299.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000463 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000463">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000383"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000389"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-17T21:07:21.633891Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Interaction in which membership of a coalition toward common interests enables it to deny success to those with conflicting interests or abilities.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">cooperative interaction</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>cooperation interaction</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;I saw 3 instances of cooperative hunting attack... The first observation, on 1 August 1977, involved 2 dark-phase jaegers and an immature female Pectoral Sandpiper (Calidris melanotos). When first seen, the jaegers were chasing the sandpiper in level flight. The jaegers pursued the sandpiper to a gravel road where it took refuge in the grass. The leading jaeger forced the sandpiper up, and after a quick series of alternate stoops by both jaegers, 1 caught the sandpiper in its bill, just above the grass. The jaeger carried the flapping sandpiper about 20 m, landed on the tundra and then placed the prey under its feet. The jaeger killed the victim by stabbing with its beak at the head and thorax region; both jaegers then plucked the sandpiper. Two min later, the first jaeger (the captor) picked up the sandpiper in its bill and allowed the second jaeger to tear bits of meat from the prey. After a few bites, the jaegers reversed roles and the first jaeger fed while the second jaeger held the sandpiper. After another 4 min and 2 more reversals, the jaegers had effectively divided up the catch.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000463"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Interaction in which membership of a coalition toward common interests enables it to deny success to those with conflicting interests or abilities.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Thus the evolution of higher levels of social integration can be an &quot;emergent&quot; result of selection on competing individuals. As stated in more general terms by Alexander and Borgia (1977), &quot;To the extent that natural selection has produced coalitions of genetic units or individuals, whose numbers and cooperative interactions toward common interests enible them to deny success to subunits with conflicting interests or abilities to reproduce differentially, the coalitions themselves may be properly described as the units of selection.&quot;&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000463"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">cooperative interaction</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1979</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000463"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>cooperation interaction</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000463"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;I saw 3 instances of cooperative hunting attack... The first observation, on 1 August 1977, involved 2 dark-phase jaegers and an immature female Pectoral Sandpiper (Calidris melanotos). When first seen, the jaegers were chasing the sandpiper in level flight. The jaegers pursued the sandpiper to a gravel road where it took refuge in the grass. The leading jaeger forced the sandpiper up, and after a quick series of alternate stoops by both jaegers, 1 caught the sandpiper in its bill, just above the grass. The jaeger carried the flapping sandpiper about 20 m, landed on the tundra and then placed the prey under its feet. The jaeger killed the victim by stabbing with its beak at the head and thorax region; both jaegers then plucked the sandpiper. Two min later, the first jaeger (the captor) picked up the sandpiper in its bill and allowed the second jaeger to tear bits of meat from the prey. After a few bites, the jaegers reversed roles and the first jaeger fed while the second jaeger held the sandpiper. After another 4 min and 2 more reversals, the jaegers had effectively divided up the catch.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/4161407</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000464 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000464">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000438"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000270"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-17T21:07:57.163515Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Influence that benefits an animal by managing another&apos;s actions.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>coercion</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">manipulation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>Machiavellian intelligence</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>manipulation influence</rdfs:label>
        <skos:narrowMatch>NBO:0020063</skos:narrowMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;For some the ‘communication as information’ vs ‘communication as manipulation’ debate is largely one of semantics, for others communication involves both information and manipulation, and for others the debate raises some fundamental ideas about how communicative interactions actually influence behavior and shape interactions and relationships among individuals.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;There are also forms of social intelligence, for example where individuals deliberately manipulate the behaviour of others by deception or reconciliation (i.e. Machiavellian intelligence).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000464"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Influence that benefits an animal by managing another&apos;s actions.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10071-014-0761-0</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000464"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>coercion</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2025</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10988-025-09438-8</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000464"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">manipulation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2015</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10071-014-0761-0</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000464"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Machiavellian intelligence</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2015</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10071-014-0761-0</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000464"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>manipulation influence</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000464"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;For some the ‘communication as information’ vs ‘communication as manipulation’ debate is largely one of semantics, for others communication involves both information and manipulation, and for others the debate raises some fundamental ideas about how communicative interactions actually influence behavior and shape interactions and relationships among individuals.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3389/fetho.2022.1066186</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000464"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;There are also forms of social intelligence, for example where individuals deliberately manipulate the behaviour of others by deception or reconciliation (i.e. Machiavellian intelligence).&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10071-014-0761-0</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000465 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000465">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000438"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MFOEM_000001"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_mental_process"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2024-11-17T21:08:50.341699Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Influence that promotes a sense of belonging in another.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>acceptance</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">affiliative relationship</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">acceptance influence</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000894"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;Ardeola ibis...
...it is disadvantageous for chicks to beg from adults indiscriminately; acceptance could result in inadequate feeding for all.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Chimpanzees...
Newly-immigrated females tend to choose, follow and form affiliative relationship with, one particular resident female by grooming her and caring for her infant.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:relatedMatch>NBO:0000281</skos:relatedMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;During maternal attendance, neonates may develop sex-specific affiliative behavior with their kin.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000465"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Influence that promotes a sense of belonging in another.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1177/1745691610369469</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;These analyses are consistent with his suggestion that there are different domain-specific motivational systems for physiological needs, safety (self-protection) needs, esteem (status) needs, and belongingness (affiliation) needs.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000465"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>acceptance</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1967</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000465"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">affiliative relationship</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1999</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1537/ase.107.141</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000465"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
