        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The head is bent sideways over a small angle and brought back again. Often this movement is repeated to the left and to the right. The centre of the movement lies close behind the operculae (atlas). As long as the movement was carried out when the fish rests in a horizontal position and the tail made no appreciable lateral movements, we call this action jerking. We observed this jerking regularly among the courtship actions of Cichlasoma, Aequidens, Hemichromis and Astronotus. In Pterphyllum we observed a similar attitude during interterritorial flights.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
