    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000523 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000523">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000081"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0002404"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000726"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/placement_by_movement"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-01-14T12:16:14.054836Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Placement that results from extending.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">extended</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>extension</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>aiming</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>erected</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym xml:lang="en">pointing</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>projected</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>extension placement</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000624"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;Most balistoid fishes possess a mobile ventral keel that can be extended and retracted.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Pufferfish (Tetraodontidae) inflate their bodies with the added effect of raising spines, which is assumed to make them more difficult to bite and/or swallow...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The awkward posturing and strange acrobatics of the oriole were similar to those described for many small species. Always there was some deflection of the tail, even during low intensity anting. Usually the tail was brought sharply around to one side of the feet, or between them, so that the tip pointed forward and the dorsal surface lay largely on the floor.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...fishes... wedging themselves into corners using their erectable dorsal spines and ventral keels.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;In the special case where the distal segments of a multiply jointed appendage are swung through a dorsal/ventral arc at a proximal joint (e.g. the pectoral girdle of a bird), our terms related to extension and flexion will fit this definition if the whole appendage (e.g the bird&apos;s wing) is being referred to. But if a continuation of either ventral or dorsal arc tucks flat against the body in one or other direction, our terms related to abduction and adduction are preferred.

NB: Beware of confusion where sources refer to protraction as extension, alongside the special case of flexion discussed above, i.e. do not assume that flexion is always the opposite of extension: a bird may at times flex or extend the humerus at the pectoral girdle (pointing away from the body) in a protracted OR a retracted wing.

NNB: Beware of confusion where sources refer to our special case of extension as retraction. This can happen where an appendage is moved through a dorsal/ventral arc but parallel to the body (alongside/parasagitally).</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
