    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000523"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In the special case where the distal segments of a multiply jointed appendage are swung through a dorsal/ventral arc at a proximal joint (e.g. the pectoral girdle of a bird), our terms related to extension and flexion will fit this definition if the whole appendage (e.g the bird&apos;s wing) is being referred to. But if a continuation of either ventral or dorsal arc tucks flat against the body in one or other direction, our terms related to abduction and adduction are preferred.

NB: Beware of confusion where sources refer to protraction as extension, alongside the special case of flexion discussed above, i.e. do not assume that flexion is always the opposite of extension: a bird may at times flex or extend the humerus at the pectoral girdle (pointing away from the body) in a protracted OR a retracted wing.

NNB: Beware of confusion where sources refer to our special case of extension as retraction. This can happen where an appendage is moved through a dorsal/ventral arc but parallel to the body (alongside/parasagitally).</owl:annotatedTarget>
