        <rdfs:comment>This is the aerial phase of a step.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000642"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>rowing pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000642"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Aerial and aquatic modes of locomotion are similar in nature due to the fact that propulsion mechanisms utilize similar characteristics of aero and hydrodynamics in each medium. The key differences between the two are the increased implications of gravity during aerial operations compared to aquatic operations, and the difference in density of the fluids, being approximately 800 times denser in water than air...

...insects typically do not use the same mechanism for locomotion in the different mediums, although many examples exist that utilize both modes of transport. To achieve this insects typically use their hexapod gaits in a rowing motion, utilizing a drag based strategy, and then subsequently use wings during aerial operations.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1088/1748-3182/9/1/011001</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000642"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Rowing animals with single (𝑛=2) pair of appendages hop and sink as opposed to swimming steadily.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://search.proquest.com/openview/28072abf0dcabdc270c0235a6fb16211/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000643 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000643">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000147"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001839"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/kinetics"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-03-30T19:32:24.164249Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Kinetics that moves an appendage in order to reduce drag.

Movement may include bending, collapsing, or retracting.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">streamlining</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>rheomorphism</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">streamlining kinetics</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...in the father lasher (Myoxocehpalus scorpius), during station holding against substrate, the ventral fin rays extend and contact the substrate, often forward of the fin base. The more dorsal fin membrane extends back to lay against the side of the body and is thought to provide streamlining.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000643"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Kinetics that moves an appendage in order to reduce drag.

Movement may include bending, collapsing, or retracting.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>https://search.proquest.com/openview/28072abf0dcabdc270c0235a6fb16211/1?pq-origsite=gscholar&amp;cbl=18750&amp;diss=y</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;This is a reasonable assumption given that metachronal swimmers bend, collapse, or retract their appendages and recover multiple appendages together in order to reduce drag via streamlining.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000643"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">streamlining</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2022</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icac061</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000643"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>rheomorphism</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000643"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">streamlining kinetics</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000643"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...in the father lasher (Myoxocehpalus scorpius), during station holding against substrate, the ventral fin rays extend and contact the substrate, often forward of the fin base. The more dorsal fin membrane extends back to lay against the side of the body and is thought to provide streamlining.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icac061</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000644 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000644">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000643"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000026"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-03-30T19:32:56.738551Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Streamlining that occurs on the recovery stroke.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">feathering</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">feathering streamlining</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...the pectoral fins of the boxfish have a temporally asymmetric beat cycle, with fin abduction being significantly slower than the adduction phase. This may indicate that the adducting fin is serving as the power stroke of the fin-beat cycle... feathering the pectoral fins during the lower speed... phase* should minimize resistance. Such propulsor movement patterns are frequently seen in drag-based systems.&quot;

* In the original, it appears that a typo has labelled this the &apos;adduction phase&apos; instead of the &apos;abduction phase&apos;.</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000644"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Streamlining that occurs on the recovery stroke.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;On the recovery stroke, the fin is feathered, the superior margin leading.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000644"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">feathering</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</dc:date>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000644"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">feathering streamlining</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000644"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...the pectoral fins of the boxfish have a temporally asymmetric beat cycle, with fin abduction being significantly slower than the adduction phase. This may indicate that the adducting fin is serving as the power stroke of the fin-beat cycle... feathering the pectoral fins during the lower speed... phase* should minimize resistance. Such propulsor movement patterns are frequently seen in drag-based systems.&quot;

* In the original, it appears that a typo has labelled this the &apos;adduction phase&apos; instead of the &apos;abduction phase&apos;.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icac061</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000645 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000645">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000147"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001413"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/kinetics"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-03-31T08:16:22.729708Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Kinetics which voluntarily manages lateral change in an organism’s trajectory.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">steering</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">steering kinetics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000174"/>
        <skos:closeMatch>NBO:0000376</skos:closeMatch>
        <skos:example>&quot;Flying squirrels and flying phalangers also utilize relatively large tails for steering and stabilization in flight.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Usually more than one method is used at a time and in such sharp turns as is seen in Salmo after having risen to a fly it is likely that as many as possible are used in combination.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...mechanically unstable bodies that can easily be blown off course are also very responsive to voluntary steering maneuvers.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000645"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000147"/>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1126/science.288.5463.100</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000645"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Kinetics which voluntarily manages lateral change in an organism’s trajectory.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1126/science.288.5463.100</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000645"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">steering</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</dc:date>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000645"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
