        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In the fastest forms the vertical fins can be depressed so completely as to mar the perfection of the streamline form in no way at all.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000577"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000272"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_activity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-04-07T06:54:10.900312Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-09-14T19:33:44.00345Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Basing that involves any kind of construction.

This could be ‘construction by subtraction’ or ‘construction by addition’, and simple or complex.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">nesting</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>bed building</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym xml:lang="en">nest building</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>nest construction</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">nesting basing</rdfs:label>
        <skos:relatedMatch>NBO:0000156</skos:relatedMatch>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...taller wire-corals may provide a greater surface area for egg laying, and hence increasing wire-coral size heightens the suitability of the host as a nest-site since B. yongei are demersal spawners that lay their eggs near the tip of their host.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...the fishes often perform only the activities of the fighting and nestbuilding motivations...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;At least 9,000 species of fish build a nest of some sort, either for laying eggs, or for shelter from predators.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Nest homeostasis provides not only an incubator for the brood, but also a thermal refuge for individuals to use temperature gradients to regulate their own body temperature.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Sociality has brought many advantages to various hymenoptera species, including their ability of regulating physical factors in their nest (e.g., temperature).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Sometimes chimpanzees carried a branch laden with fruit to a day nest or large trunk where they could lie, or fed lethargically on nearby fruit while lying in a day bed.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The techniques with which birds build their nests have also been described in some detail and are also various: they range from the sculpting of burrows or cavities from substrate excavation, through the moulding of mud or salivary mucus by vibrating head and/or shaping breast and feet movements, the piling up of materials where subsequent bill manipulations, coupled with side-to-side shaking movements, may be made in order to entangle or intertwine nest components, to the weaving of hanging nest baskets using intricate tuck, looping, interlocking, winding, and knotting bill-made stitches to fasten and secure grassy materials.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Basing that involves any kind of construction.

This could be ‘construction by subtraction’ or ‘construction by addition’, and simple or complex.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1098/rstb.2022.0138</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;However, it is known that mammals construct a variety of structures many of which involve burrowing into the ground to form a network of tunnels, described by Hancocks as ‘construction by subtraction’. These burrows may be lined with plant materials simply piled up in chambers, which may provide physical comfort or affect the microenvironment in the burrow, but often such structures are resting sites. This review is not, however, concerned with the organization of these relatively simple nest structures but rather it focuses on ‘construction by addition’, where the nest involves production of a structure following some form of construction behaviour by the individual.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">nesting</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1922</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/2939673</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>bed building</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2020</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1002/ece3.6372</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...the nest, that is, the zone where the bear gathers vegetative material to build a bed.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">nest building</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>nest construction</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1966</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/4083155</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">nesting basing</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...taller wire-corals may provide a greater surface area for egg laying, and hence increasing wire-coral size heightens the suitability of the host as a nest-site since B. yongei are demersal spawners that lay their eggs near the tip of their host.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://www.science.nus.edu.sg/wp-content/uploads/sites/11/2024/05/s22rbz145-157.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...the fishes often perform only the activities of the fighting and nestbuilding motivations...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;At least 9,000 species of fish build a nest of some sort, either for laying eggs, or for shelter from predators.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10071-014-0761-0</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Nest homeostasis provides not only an incubator for the brood, but also a thermal refuge for individuals to use temperature gradients to regulate their own body temperature.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0200048</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Sociality has brought many advantages to various hymenoptera species, including their ability of regulating physical factors in their nest (e.g., temperature).&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0200048</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Sometimes chimpanzees carried a branch laden with fruit to a day nest or large trunk where they could lie, or fed lethargically on nearby fruit while lying in a day bed.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1002/ajpa.1330870108</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000657"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The techniques with which birds build their nests have also been described in some detail and are also various: they range from the sculpting of burrows or cavities from substrate excavation, through the moulding of mud or salivary mucus by vibrating head and/or shaping breast and feet movements, the piling up of materials where subsequent bill manipulations, coupled with side-to-side shaking movements, may be made in order to entangle or intertwine nest components, to the weaving of hanging nest baskets using intricate tuck, looping, interlocking, winding, and knotting bill-made stitches to fasten and secure grassy materials.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.3819/ccbr.2016.110005</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000658 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000658">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000475"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000659"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
