        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-04T13:43:36.412855Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Homeostasis that affects weight in water by the lift from a low density inclusion.

This may be a gas or lipid.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>buoyancy control</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">hydrostatic function</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>buoyancy homeostasis</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;In contrast to Thamnophis sirtalis, another generalist garter snake capable of capturing aquatic prey, T. elegans appeared comfortable when fully immersed (i.e. it did not avoid complete submersion and regularly engaged in sustained bouts of swimming) and appeared to have greater buoyancy control.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Negatively buoyant benthic gobies survive extreme hypoxia by performing aquatic surface respiration (ASR) in shallow water.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000677"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Homeostasis that affects weight in water by the lift from a low density inclusion.

This may be a gas or lipid.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.198.1.79</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000677"/>
