        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/j.1420-9101.2009.01826.x</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000683"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The male then steps onto his mate&apos;s back, grasps the humerous region of each wing in his feet, begins to perform rhythmic treading movements which gradually accelerate, then lowers his tail to achieve cloacal contact and simultaneously begins to flap in order to retain balance. In 16% of 25 copulations the male grasped the female&apos;s nape in his beak.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000683"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;If the weight of the snake is not balanced when moving on shallow cylindrical surfaces, the snake will topple sideways. Actively gripping cylindrical surfaces reduces both of these problems. However, some snakes actually take advantage of the forces from their weight by using it as a propulsive mechanism for moving down steep surfaces while holding a static posture and exerting a slight grip to control the speed of sliding.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icaa017</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000174"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000653"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/manoeuvre"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2026-07-27T18:41:58.706354Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Turning in which rolling through the turn, the body and fins canted at an angle to the incident water flow, can aid in the generation of a centripetal force to reduce slip.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>bank the body</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>banked turn</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>banking maneuver</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>inward bank</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>outward bank</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">bank turning</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...blue whales take advantage of their dorsal flexibility to perform turns by banking... up to 90° inward to perform fast turns and their turning speed is strongly correlated with bank angle.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The rays that were observed swam by oscillatory movements of the pectoral fins (Aetobatus narinari, Myliobatis freminvillii and Rhinoptera bonasus) or by undulations of the fins (Dasyatis americana and Dasyatis centroura). Oscillatory rays maneuvered by either powered or unpowered gliding turns, whereas undulatory rays turned using asymmetrical undulations of both pectoral fins. Underwater observations showed that both rays used banking while turning, where bank angle ranged from 15–70°.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Turning in which rolling through the turn, the body and fins canted at an angle to the incident water flow, can aid in the generation of a centripetal force to reduce slip.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1016/j.jembe.2011.03.010</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Movement of the paired pectoral fins of rays can aid in the generation of a centripetal force to bank the body and reduce slip. Banking is a rolling maneuver that provides a greater projected area. By banking through the turn, the body and fins would be canted at an angle to the incident water flow (i.e., angle of attack).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>bank the body</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2011</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.jembe.2011.03.010</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>banked turn</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2019</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icy121</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>banking maneuver</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2019</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icy121</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>inward bank</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2019</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icy121</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>outward bank</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2019</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icy121</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...outward banked...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">bank turning</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2011</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.jembe.2011.03.010</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...banking while turning...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...blue whales take advantage of their dorsal flexibility to perform turns by banking... up to 90° inward to perform fast turns and their turning speed is strongly correlated with bank angle.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1093/icb/icy121</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000684"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The rays that were observed swam by oscillatory movements of the pectoral fins (Aetobatus narinari, Myliobatis freminvillii and Rhinoptera bonasus) or by undulations of the fins (Dasyatis americana and Dasyatis centroura). Oscillatory rays maneuvered by either powered or unpowered gliding turns, whereas undulatory rays turned using asymmetrical undulations of both pectoral fins. Underwater observations showed that both rays used banking while turning, where bank angle ranged from 15–70°.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.jembe.2011.03.010</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000685 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000685">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000013"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000653"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/manoeuvre"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-07T07:18:48.718523Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Rotating that manoeuvres from side to side, and from upright to upside down.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>roll</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">rolling</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>side-to-side</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>capsizing</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>corkscrewing</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>spiraling</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>whirling</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>rolling rotating</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;In the more complex method of grasping a worm the fish rolls over onto one side, simultaneously forming an S-shape ; the head points down at the worm, and the dorsal fin is usually spread as much as the body curvature allows. After briefly fixing on the prey the Badis badis lunges and seizes the worm.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Large sturgeon when swimming slowly, especially when starting off, have a noticeable roll...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Much commoner are the oil-distributing movements in which head and neck are vigorously rubbed and rolled over the back and area around the humerus.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Larval zebrafish can actively rotate their bodies by selective contractions of the dorsal (epaxial) and ventral (hypaxial) trunk musculature. Differential contraction of the dorsal and ventral muscles on the right and left sides of the body subserves roll&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000685"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Rotating that manoeuvres from side to side, and from upright to upside down.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9780226315737</dc:source>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/jfb.12866</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Side-to-side rolling motions and pitching over onto the back are common body motions observed during feeding in rorqual whales.&quot; (ISBN:9780226315737)</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Typically, fish that exhibited this behaviour would repeatedly swim on their sides before recovering to their normal upright swimming position.&quot; (doi:10.1111/jfb.12866)</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000685"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>roll</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
