        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/kinetics"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-12T06:28:37.076084Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Kinetics that exerts a contact force towards the subject.

It may be countered by a pushing force from the same subject.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>pull</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">pulling</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>tug</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>dragging</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>tearing</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>pull kinetics</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000736"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;In Tephritidae, disengagement of the coupling occurs as the male dismounts and moves away end-to-end, pulling the phallus from the female aculeus.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;It was really curious to note in cases of difficulty, as when two pieces of comb met at an angle, how often the bees would entirely pull down and rebuild in different ways the same cell, sometimes recurring to a shape which they had at first rejected.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The chelae are often not lifted completely free from the substrate during retraction; consequently, they will leave a gouged-out groove in the centre of the crab track as they are dragged along the sand.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000696"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Kinetics that exerts a contact force towards the subject.

It may be countered by a pushing force from the same subject.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/BF02381373</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
