        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1990</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/BF00751035</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000697"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>gouging</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2020</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/1365-2435.13457</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000697"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">scrape graze</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000697"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The scrapers... functional group encompasses... Scarus... altipinnis, S. chameleon, S. dimidiatus, S. flavipectoralis... and Hipposcarus longiceps.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/BF00751035</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://humanbehaviourchange.org/ontology/BCIO_050416"/>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/historical_disposition"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-12T18:51:13.54063Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Behavioural disposition that is realized when a constructed initial causal factor for behaviour is directed towards some goal object.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">motivation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>HISTORICAL TERM

This term is part of a historical ethological perspective which is no longer mainstream.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">motivation-initiator</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000706"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000707"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;Aggregating between two gravid females is clear cut. The females, though, must be deprived of contact with a male. Such females will then spend most of their time together, only separating occasionally. Infrequently they may fight, but this almost never proceeds beyond initial displays. Two not sexually motivated, on the other hand, will enter into well developed fights. Thus the aggregating of two gravid, male-deprived females probably results from substituting one another for a female and is made possible through the suppression of fighting behavior.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Most of these terms have not disappeared from usage completely, although as psychology decreased its focus on behaviour and increased its focus on cognition, the field of motivation has become ever more unfashionable...

...there are several common ways to split these constructs further... The first is the concept of primary (inherited, innate) and secondary (or acquired, derived, learned) motivations. Although informative in many species, what is innate in one species may be learned in another, and this distinction is further blurred by processes such as imprinting and tendencies to learn particular things. Related divisions have been made between viscerogenic/biogenic (from the body) and psychogenic (from the mind), biological and social behaviours, and intrinsic and extrinsic motivation; all having a similar vagueness about them. Behaviour is also sometimes divided up by how it is controlled, between simple reflexes, learned habits, and deliberative goal-directed behaviour, however, even within the same individual, the same behaviour can be controlled by different levels during different times and situations...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Behavioural disposition that is realized when a constructed initial causal factor for behaviour is directed towards some goal object.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1177/10597123211040574</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;These kinds of categorisation are varied in their approach, the most common of which is the enumeration of types of initiators of behaviour through various constructs, the most prominent three of which bear the names of ‘instincts’, ‘drives’ and ‘needs’. These are the initial causal factors that motivate behaviour and are directed towards some goal object, and we will briefly review each of these constructs in turn.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">motivation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">motivation-initiator</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Aggregating between two gravid females is clear cut. The females, though, must be deprived of contact with a male. Such females will then spend most of their time together, only separating occasionally. Infrequently they may fight, but this almost never proceeds beyond initial displays. Two not sexually motivated, on the other hand, will enter into well developed fights. Thus the aggregating of two gravid, male-deprived females probably results from substituting one another for a female and is made possible through the suppression of fighting behavior.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
        <rdfs:comment>This example is given, not to promote the case, but to illustrate the type of analysis which motivation-based ethological concepts once led to.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Most of these terms have not disappeared from usage completely, although as psychology decreased its focus on behaviour and increased its focus on cognition, the field of motivation has become ever more unfashionable...

...there are several common ways to split these constructs further... The first is the concept of primary (inherited, innate) and secondary (or acquired, derived, learned) motivations. Although informative in many species, what is innate in one species may be learned in another, and this distinction is further blurred by processes such as imprinting and tendencies to learn particular things. Related divisions have been made between viscerogenic/biogenic (from the body) and psychogenic (from the mind), biological and social behaviours, and intrinsic and extrinsic motivation; all having a similar vagueness about them. Behaviour is also sometimes divided up by how it is controlled, between simple reflexes, learned habits, and deliberative goal-directed behaviour, however, even within the same individual, the same behaviour can be controlled by different levels during different times and situations...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1177/10597123211040574</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000699 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000699">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000376"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000311"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_activity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-13T11:34:20.112527Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Avoidance in which an animal presses its posterior part against the rear-most region of its shelter, readies propulsors, and becomes almost motionless.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>cornered</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">nestling</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>at bay</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>cornered avoidance</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...the presence of an emergency escape hatch near the surface... allows mice with that ex tended phenotype to rapidly tunnel to the surface if cornered in their burrow by a predator.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Badis badis...
Nestling... The fish backs up while seemingly probing for a crevice with its tail. Then it presses its body, especially the posterior part, against the rear-most region of the shelter where the light intensity is low. The median and pelvic fins are folded until the fish assumes a &quot;satisfactory position. Then these fins are usually spread, and the fish becomes almost motionless.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000699"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Avoidance in which an animal presses its posterior part against the rear-most region of its shelter, readies propulsors, and becomes almost motionless.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000699"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>cornered</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2021</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2021.09.082</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000699"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">nestling</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</dc:date>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000699"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>at bay</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1998</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1002/(SICI)1098-2337(1998)24:3&lt;197::AID-AB4&gt;3.0.CO;2-I</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000699"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>cornered avoidance</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000699"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...the presence of an emergency escape hatch near the surface... allows mice with that ex tended phenotype to rapidly tunnel to the surface if cornered in their burrow by a predator.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1016/j.cub.2021.09.082</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000699"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Badis badis...
Nestling... The fish backs up while seemingly probing for a crevice with its tail. Then it presses its body, especially the posterior part, against the rear-most region of the shelter where the light intensity is low. The median and pelvic fins are folded until the fish assumes a &quot;satisfactory position. Then these fins are usually spread, and the fish becomes almost motionless.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000700 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000700">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000122"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ZFA_0000555"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-13T14:16:02.693841Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Gaping that rhythmically opens and closes the gill covers, with variable, situation dependant tempo and amplitude.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">opercular beat</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>opercular movement</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>opercular pumping</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>operculum gaping</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Courtship displays of R. decemdigitatus consisted of 11 steps: ... (ii) increase in opercular movements...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...stress-related freezing (lack of body movements except for eyes, with increased opercular movements) can be differentiated from sleep-like/resting behavior (lack of body movement with reduced opercular activity.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...while swimming to pump water in and out...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000700"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Gaping that rhythmically opens and closes the gill covers, with variable, situation dependant tempo and amplitude.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000700"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">opercular beat</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</dc:date>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000700"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>opercular movement</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</dc:date>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000700"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>opercular pumping</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1995</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.198.1.79</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000700"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>operculum gaping</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000700"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Courtship displays of R. decemdigitatus consisted of 11 steps: ... (ii) increase in opercular movements...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/s10682-007-9183-7</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000700"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...stress-related freezing (lack of body movements except for eyes, with increased opercular movements) can be differentiated from sleep-like/resting behavior (lack of body movement with reduced opercular activity.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1089/zeb.2012.0861</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000700"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...while swimming to pump water in and out...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000701 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000701">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698"/>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/historical_disposition"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-13T17:15:14.431263Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Initiator which is constructed from a pattern of behaviour which is inherited and specific.

It may be characterized by the accumulation of reaction specific energy or specific action potential (SAP) which is the state of the animal responsible for its readiness to perform the behaviour patterns of one instinct in preference to all other behaviour patterns. An innate releasing mechanism (IRM)  is selectively responsive to a special stimulus situation. Releasers are structures or actions that emanate sign stimuli which are perceived by an animal.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>innate behaviour</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">instinct</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>HISTORICAL TERM

This term is part of a historical ethological perspective which is no longer mainstream.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>instinct initiator</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000773"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;It may be difficult, but we ought to admire the savage instinctive hatred of the queen-bee, which urges her instantly to destroy the young queens her daughters as soon as born, or to perish herself in the combat;&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000701"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Initiator which is constructed from a pattern of behaviour which is inherited and specific.

It may be characterized by the accumulation of reaction specific energy or specific action potential (SAP) which is the state of the animal responsible for its readiness to perform the behaviour patterns of one instinct in preference to all other behaviour patterns. An innate releasing mechanism (IRM)  is selectively responsive to a special stimulus situation. Releasers are structures or actions that emanate sign stimuli which are perceived by an animal.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/4083155</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000701"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>innate behaviour</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000701"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">instinct</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1859</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000701"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>instinct initiator</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000701"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;It may be difficult, but we ought to admire the savage instinctive hatred of the queen-bee, which urges her instantly to destroy the young queens her daughters as soon as born, or to perish herself in the combat;&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000702 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000702">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000701"/>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/historical_disposition"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-13T17:16:04.40902Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Instinct initiator that is an introductory phase.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">appetitive behaviour</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>HISTORICAL TERM

This term is part of a historical ethological perspective which is no longer mainstream.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">appetitive initiator</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...a Peregrine Falcon (Falco peregrinus) searching for food begins by randomly roaming over its hunting territory. This appetitive behavior continues until a stimulus situation is encountered (e.g., a flock of teal executing fligh&apos;t maneuvers).&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;LORENZ has shown that the animal actively strives to find the stimuli that makes the releasive mechanism of a certain pattern work... LORENZ, following CRAIG, called this striving or purposive behaviour appetitive behaviour. This appetitive behaviour is the place where learning and intelligence can play an important part.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000702"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Instinct initiator that is an introductory phase.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.2307/4083155</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The variable introductory phase of an instinctive behaviour pattern or sequence is called appetitive behaviour.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000702"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">appetitive behaviour</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000702"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">appetitive initiator</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000702"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...a Peregrine Falcon (Falco peregrinus) searching for food begins by randomly roaming over its hunting territory. This appetitive behavior continues until a stimulus situation is encountered (e.g., a flock of teal executing fligh&apos;t maneuvers).&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/4083155</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000702"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;LORENZ has shown that the animal actively strives to find the stimuli that makes the releasive mechanism of a certain pattern work... LORENZ, following CRAIG, called this striving or purposive behaviour appetitive behaviour. This appetitive behaviour is the place where learning and intelligence can play an important part.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000703 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000703">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000701"/>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/historical_disposition"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-13T17:16:16.171639Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Instinct initiator that is a terminating act.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">consummatory action</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>HISTORICAL TERM

This term is part of a historical ethological perspective which is no longer mainstream.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">consummatory initiator</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...a Peregrine Falcon (Falco peregrinus) searching for food begins by randomly roaming over its hunting territory... The final stoop, capture, kill, plucking, and eating form a relatively simple and stereotyped chain of consummatory acts.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000703"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Instinct initiator that is a terminating act.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.2307/4083155</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...the act terminating a behavior pattern or sequence is the consummatory act.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000703"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">consummatory action</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000703"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">consummatory initiator</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000703"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...a Peregrine Falcon (Falco peregrinus) searching for food begins by randomly roaming over its hunting territory... The final stoop, capture, kill, plucking, and eating form a relatively simple and stereotyped chain of consummatory acts.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/4083155</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000704 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000704">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698"/>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/historical_disposition"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-13T17:20:51.554481Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Initiator which is constructed from the complex of internal and external states and stimuli leading to a given behaviour.

This term may include the stimuli, specific action potential, and innate releasing mechanisms involved in a given behavioral act.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">drive</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>tendency</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>HISTORICAL TERM

This term is part of a historical ethological perspective which is no longer mainstream.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>drive initiator</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://humanbehaviourchange.org/ontology/BCIO_050783"/>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000704"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Initiator which is constructed from the complex of internal and external states and stimuli leading to a given behaviour.

This term may include the stimuli, specific action potential, and innate releasing mechanisms involved in a given behavioral act.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.2307/4083155</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Drive has been defined by Thorpe (1951) as &quot;the complex of internal and external states and stimuli leading to a given behaviour.&quot;&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000704"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">drive</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000704"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>tendency</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1966</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/4083155</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Because of the difficulties of determining internal states without experimentation, the term tendency has been used to indicate the readiness to show a particular type of behaviour as observed under natural conditions.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000704"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>drive initiator</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000705 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000705">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698"/>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/historical_disposition"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-13T20:20:29.347051Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Initiator which is constructed from goal objects with some kind of biological, environmental or sociological significance.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">needs</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>HISTORICAL TERM

This term is part of a historical ethological perspective which is no longer mainstream.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>needs initiator</rdfs:label>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;For many homeostatic resources (like water and oxygen), the organism will die in the long run if a subsistence level of consumption is not maintained, and it is in this sense that the organism needs these resources. However, the same cannot be said for the vast majority of observed behaviours, where the behaviour is not essential for either survival or reproduction, but rather is opportunistic or has instrumental value, facilitating other goal objectives.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000705"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Initiator which is constructed from goal objects with some kind of biological, environmental or sociological significance.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1177/10597123211040574</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000705"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">needs</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2010</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1177/1745691610369469</dc:source>
        <rdfs:comment>Left in the plural form because of historical usage.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000705"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>HISTORICAL TERM

This term is part of a historical ethological perspective which is no longer mainstream.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1177/10597123211040574</dc:source>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1177/1745691610369469</dc:source>
        <rdfs:comment>These sources may entice a future editor to patch or further unravel this term.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000705"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>needs initiator</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000705"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;For many homeostatic resources (like water and oxygen), the organism will die in the long run if a subsistence level of consumption is not maintained, and it is in this sense that the organism needs these resources. However, the same cannot be said for the vast majority of observed behaviours, where the behaviour is not essential for either survival or reproduction, but rather is opportunistic or has instrumental value, facilitating other goal objectives.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1177/10597123211040574</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000706 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000706">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000553"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000694"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-05-14T06:22:38.893479Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Condition switch that performs a behaviour pattern out of the context of behaviour with which it is normally related.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym xml:lang="en">displacement</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">displacement switch</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Wing-touch is obviously a preening movement, since no other activity involves contact between beak and wing. The display contains all the characteristics of a displacement activity, namely an irrelevant movement...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:relatedMatch>NBO:0020255</skos:relatedMatch>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000706"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Condition switch that performs a behaviour pattern out of the context of behaviour with which it is normally related.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.2307/4083155</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;In other use it refers to the performance of a behavior pattern out of the context of
behavior with which it is normally related.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000706"/>
