    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000764 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000764">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000795"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002100"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-07-29T09:52:26.944859Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Shake that progresses along the body.

In water birds it is usually from tail to head.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">body-shake</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">body-shaking</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">body shake</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;In the Mallard, the shake begins with a vigorous lateral tail-wag and at the same time many of the body feathers become erected. The shake moves forward to the wings, which are vibrated rapidly but without being removed from their &quot;pockets&quot; in the flank feathers. the wing-shaking dies down, the head is lifted from its normal position and is rotated around its long axis several times while pointing upward to make an angle of about 45 degrees with the ground. During the head movement, the feathers of the back, neck, and head are erected. A secondary wing-shaking may occur while the head is being moved. The head then returns to its normal position, and the feathers all over the body are depressed to the usual resting state. A final tail-wag usually follows. The whole movement takes a few seconds to perform, the longest part often being the introductory tail-wag.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Shaking may occur during hostile encounters. This is particularly striking in aggressive situations in the White-fronted Goose, but shaking involving the wings only is also very common in this species.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The main features of the shake - feather-erection, progression of the movement along the body and rotation of the head - are universal... The primary function of the body-shake seems to be the removal of waterdrops from the bird&apos;s feathers, which is achieved by the centrifugal effect of the movements.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000764"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Shake that progresses along the body.

In water birds it is usually from tail to head.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000764"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">body-shake</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000764"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">body-shaking</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000764"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In the Mallard, the shake begins with a vigorous lateral tail-wag and at the same time many of the body feathers become erected. The shake moves forward to the wings, which are vibrated rapidly but without being removed from their &quot;pockets&quot; in the flank feathers. the wing-shaking dies down, the head is lifted from its normal position and is rotated around its long axis several times while pointing upward to make an angle of about 45 degrees with the ground. During the head movement, the feathers of the back, neck, and head are erected. A secondary wing-shaking may occur while the head is being moved. The head then returns to its normal position, and the feathers all over the body are depressed to the usual resting state. A final tail-wag usually follows. The whole movement takes a few seconds to perform, the longest part often being the introductory tail-wag.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000764"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Shaking may occur during hostile encounters. This is particularly striking in aggressive situations in the White-fronted Goose, but shaking involving the wings only is also very common in this species.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000764"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The main features of the shake - feather-erection, progression of the movement along the body and rotation of the head - are universal... The primary function of the body-shake seems to be the removal of waterdrops from the bird&apos;s feathers, which is achieved by the centrifugal effect of the movements.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000765 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000765">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000795"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000023"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002328"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-07-29T11:06:52.482784Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Shake in which the wings are shaken vigorously.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">shaking of wings</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>wing-shake</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>wing-shaking</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>wing-beat</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>wing shake</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000757"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000766"/>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000769"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;Wing-shaking is given by White-fronted Geese, Bar-headed Geese, and Barnacle Geese while threatening with the head and neck stretched out toward and opponent.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;I have also seen vigorous wing-shaking during oiling in a Semipalmated Plover (Charadrius hiaticula) and a Song Thrush (Turdus ericetorum).&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The Magpie Goose performs Wing-shaking as part of a display. The male and female of a pair walk around each other calling, erecting the feathers on the back, and repeatedly shaking the wings vigorously.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000765"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Shake in which the wings are shaken vigorously.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The wings are shaken more vigorously, and it is mainly the secondaries which are involved.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000765"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">shaking of wings</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1957</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://digitalcommons.usf.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=5800&amp;context=wilson_bulletin</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000765"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>wing-shake</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000765"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>wing-shaking</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
        <dc:source rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
