        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000767"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The Maned Goose gives very rapid Head-shakes when calling with crest erect and head held high; possibly this display had its origin in pre-flight behaviour, but it provides an example of the incorporation of head-shaking in a highly ritualized pattern.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000768 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000768">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000795"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002415"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002497"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-07-29T14:16:35.154278Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Shake in which the tail is moved from side to side.

[In birds] Tail-wagging normally follows every activity which involves, or could involve, wetting of the tail.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">tail wagging</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">tail-wagging</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>tail-wag shake</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Courtship in the genus Rhyacotriton... begins with an approach phase in which the male nudges the female. The male then faces away from the female and performs a peculiar tail-wagging display in which the tail is curled upwards over the male’s back and the tail tip is wagged back and forth.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;I have seen repeated, rapid bursts of tail-wagging performed by a female Blue-winged Teal, accompanied by day-old ducklings, as she threatened with open bill toward a male swimming nearby.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;In addition, it occurs at the beginning of the body-shake and after it, after wing-flap on land, after stretching and many preening movements on land. It often precedes and/or follows defaecation. After copulation, the male often tail-wags repeatedly; in Mallards, the penis may protrude for some time, and tail-wags are given until it is withdrawn.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The action consists of vigorous body undulations, the propulsive force of which is checked by beating the pectorals forwards.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000768"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Shake in which the tail is moved from side to side.

[In birds] Tail-wagging normally follows every activity which involves, or could involve, wetting of the tail.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The tail, which may be fanned out to some extent, is shaken from side to side several times....

Tail-wagging normally follows every activity which involves, or could involve, wetting of the tail.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000768"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">tail wagging</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000768"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">tail-wagging</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000768"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>tail-wag shake</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000768"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Courtship in the genus Rhyacotriton... begins with an approach phase in which the male nudges the female. The male then faces away from the female and performs a peculiar tail-wagging display in which the tail is curled upwards over the male’s back and the tail tip is wagged back and forth.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>ISBN:9781482294347</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000768"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;I have seen repeated, rapid bursts of tail-wagging performed by a female Blue-winged Teal, accompanied by day-old ducklings, as she threatened with open bill toward a male swimming nearby.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000768"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In addition, it occurs at the beginning of the body-shake and after it, after wing-flap on land, after stretching and many preening movements on land. It often precedes and/or follows defaecation. After copulation, the male often tail-wags repeatedly; in Mallards, the penis may protrude for some time, and tail-wags are given until it is withdrawn.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000768"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The action consists of vigorous body undulations, the propulsive force of which is checked by beating the pectorals forwards.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000769 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000769">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000795"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000023"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002327"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-07-29T14:21:24.800476Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Shake in which the wings are fully opened and flapped as in flight a few times, and then the animal subsides.

The body may be poised almost vertically.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">wing-flap</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">wing-flapping</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>wing-flap shake</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;The clearest case of ritualization of the wing-flap occur in the male pre-copulatory behaviour of the Common Eider and King Eider. The male rises on the water, opens the wings and remains briefly poised with the wings open and slightly back; the bill points upward... and two rapid flaps are given before the bird returns to a normal swimming position.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The frequency is particularly high in the least aquatic species (Spur-winged Goose, Hawaiian Goose, Magpie Goose) which are liable to become very wet during bathing.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The normal wing-flap occurs almost invariably after bathing, its frequency depending on the intensity of the bathing.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000769"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Shake in which the wings are fully opened and flapped as in flight a few times, and then the animal subsides.

The body may be poised almost vertically.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;On water, a Mallard paddles vigorously until the body is poised almost vertically, the wings are fully opened and flapped as in flight a few times. Anatidae do not leave the water to fly up a few inches during wing-flapping as many Gulls and Waders do. After flapping, the bird subsides on the water...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000769"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">wing-flap</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000769"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">wing-flapping</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000769"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>wing-flap shake</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000769"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The clearest case of ritualization of the wing-flap occur in the male pre-copulatory behaviour of the Common Eider and King Eider. The male rises on the water, opens the wings and remains briefly poised with the wings open and slightly back; the bill points upward... and two rapid flaps are given before the bird returns to a normal swimming position.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000769"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The frequency is particularly high in the least aquatic species (Spur-winged Goose, Hawaiian Goose, Magpie Goose) which are liable to become very wet during bathing.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000769"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The normal wing-flap occurs almost invariably after bathing, its frequency depending on the intensity of the bathing.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000770 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000770">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000795"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0002103"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/sub_activity_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-07-29T14:21:51.884192Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Shake in which a foot is moved rapidly forward and back.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">foot-shake</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">foot-shaking</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">foot shake</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Oxyura and Aythya species, in which the legs are spaced very far apart, roll over on one side while swimming and the foot is shaken high in the air so that it appears to emerge from the bird&apos;s back.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000770"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Shake in which a foot is moved rapidly forward and back.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;A Mallard which is standing or sitting on the ground shakes one foot rapidly forward and back a number of times and then swings it forward into the flank feathers.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000770"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">foot-shake</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000770"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">foot-shaking</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000770"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Oxyura and Aythya species, in which the legs are spaced very far apart, roll over on one side while swimming and the foot is shaken high in the air so that it appears to emerge from the bird&apos;s back.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000771 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000771">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000421"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000022"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000689"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/movement"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-07-29T14:40:10.113993Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Oscillation in which feathers are continuously splaying and furling, with gradually decreasing intensity.

Wing-shuffling and tail-fanning have the function of aiding in drying the feathers.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>shuffle</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">shuffling</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>tail-fan</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>wing-shuffle</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>shuffling oscillation</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000757"/>
        <skos:example>&quot;A most unusual account of birds using smoke concerns the small flock of Red-browed Waxbills (Estrilda temporalis) at Kairi, on Atherton Tableland, North Queensland, which Givens (1945) observed on several days in June... Each bird would stand upright, with tail as “support” and wings drooping a little forward and downward, and begin sweeping the head forward and down under the wings, meanwhile vigorously shuffling its wings and body feathers and “often toppling backward from the violence of its efforts.”&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;In Denrocygna, the wing movements are particularly rapid and are restricted to the secondaries and scapulars, giving the effect of a rocking motion of the back.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Wing-shuffling occurs during the oiling and nibbling preening which follows bathing.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000771"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Oscillation in which feathers are continuously splaying and furling, with gradually decreasing intensity.

Wing-shuffling and tail-fanning have the function of aiding in drying the feathers.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000771"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>shuffle</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1965</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853965x00138</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
