    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000397"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0015002"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-08-13T07:33:37.108639Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Routine pattern which is one of a set of low level routines available for use by a higher level routine.

Techniques may be repeated and exchanged within a single routine, depending on circumstances.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>method</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">subroutine</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>technique</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>approach</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>technique pattern</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;After briefly fixing on the prey the Badis badis lunges and seizes the worm.... The upright approach is often used in catching the first worm. However, if both the upright and on-the-side approaches occur in the same feeding bout the on-the-side technique more often appears first and subsequently is replaced by the upright method.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The feeding subroutine can be described at many levels. Λ8 is the whole activity, including preparation of going into water and drying of feathers afterwards. Λ7 is the smaller component bout within the feeding activity, that involves identifying the food, diving and retrieving and then returning to the surface. This is repeated several times before the individual returns to the perch to dry. Since the focal goal of eating, Λ3, actually gets consummated in the middle of the activity, everything after is not actually necessary to this goal, but rather ‘clears up’ many of the temporary state changes that were necessary for reaching that goal.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Routine pattern which is one of a set of low level routines available for use by a higher level routine.

Techniques may be repeated and exchanged within a single routine, depending on circumstances.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1177/10597123211040574</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>method</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</dc:date>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">subroutine</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2022</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">doi:10.1177/10597123211040574</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>technique</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</dc:date>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>approach</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1961</dc:date>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>technique pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;After briefly fixing on the prey the Badis badis lunges and seizes the worm.... The upright approach is often used in catching the first worm. However, if both the upright and on-the-side approaches occur in the same feeding bout the on-the-side technique more often appears first and subsequently is replaced by the upright method.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>http://www.nativefishlab.net/library/textpdf/15582.pdf</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000801"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;The feeding subroutine can be described at many levels. Λ8 is the whole activity, including preparation of going into water and drying of feathers afterwards. Λ7 is the smaller component bout within the feeding activity, that involves identifying the food, diving and retrieving and then returning to the surface. This is repeated several times before the individual returns to the perch to dry. Since the focal goal of eating, Λ3, actually gets consummated in the middle of the activity, everything after is not actually necessary to this goal, but rather ‘clears up’ many of the temporary state changes that were necessary for reaching that goal.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1177/10597123211040574</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000802 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000802">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000743"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001910"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/movement"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-08-13T11:09:50.357664Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Contracting in which tissues fold at right angles to the contraction.

Buckling limits the contracting.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">buckling</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">buckling contracting</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;This phenomenon of buckling seems to be characteristic of all the muscle sheets of Metridium. Where specific effectors such as the marginal sphincter or the mesenteric retractors are developed, over-growth of the muscle layer leads to the very great folding so familiar in sections of sea-anemones. Much of this folding is permanent and cannot be smoothed out during expansion of the animal but it is essentially only a special case of the buckling phenomenon.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000802"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Contracting in which tissues fold at right angles to the contraction.

Buckling limits the contracting.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.27.3.264</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000802"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">buckling</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.27.3.264</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000802"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">buckling contracting</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000802"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;This phenomenon of buckling seems to be characteristic of all the muscle sheets of Metridium. Where specific effectors such as the marginal sphincter or the mesenteric retractors are developed, over-growth of the muscle layer leads to the very great folding so familiar in sections of sea-anemones. Much of this folding is permanent and cannot be smoothed out during expansion of the animal but it is essentially only a special case of the buckling phenomenon.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1242/jeb.27.3.264</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000803 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000803">
