        <dc:source>https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000837"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">relieving</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000837"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>relief cooperation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000837"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...fishes... relieving each other when guarding the young.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000837"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;In general, the mates alternately lay and fertilize and when relieving each other they usually display and give a formalized tail beat.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000698"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001558"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-09-15T10:07:36.588495Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Initiator in which an animal starts to perform a movement but does not complete it.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>impulse reversal</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>incipient</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym xml:lang="en">intention movement</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>preparatory</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>intention initiator</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Another may be seen in Amiatus which at times will start a series of waves in the dorsal at its anterior end preparatory to moving forward, but before the inertia is overcome, some negative impulse causes the initiation of a similar series from the posterior end, effectively checking the reaction of the first; these opposing series meet at about half way between, and then die out.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;We suggest that the agitated milling behavior just prior to the spawning rush excited the potential predator and the rapid upward dash that followed elicited an attack response. Close observations of the piscivores Synodus ulae and Sebasticus marmoratus disclosed that during the bobbing stage, these predators remained motionless, but while the labrids were milling, &quot;nervous&quot; twitches of the caudal fins of the predators were common. Also during milling, S. marmoratus often moved slightly forward, and S. ulae frequently &quot;danced&quot; in its pelvic fins. At the first moment of the upward rush, these predators dashed forward toward the spawning aggregations from distances varying from 60cm to 3m.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;They indicate the inclination of the animal to perform a definite activity.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Initiator in which an animal starts to perform a movement but does not complete it.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.2307/4083155</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Animals sometimes start to perform a movement but do not complete it. Heinroth termed these intention movements because they indicate to the observer what the animal is going to do.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>impulse reversal</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1926</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...reversal of the impulse...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>incipient</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">intention movement</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1966</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.2307/4083155</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>preparatory</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>intention initiator</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Another may be seen in Amiatus which at times will start a series of waves in the dorsal at its anterior end preparatory to moving forward, but before the inertia is overcome, some negative impulse causes the initiation of a similar series from the posterior end, effectively checking the reaction of the first; these opposing series meet at about half way between, and then die out.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/p.203769</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;We suggest that the agitated milling behavior just prior to the spawning rush excited the potential predator and the rapid upward dash that followed elicited an attack response. Close observations of the piscivores Synodus ulae and Sebasticus marmoratus disclosed that during the bobbing stage, these predators remained motionless, but while the labrids were milling, &quot;nervous&quot; twitches of the caudal fins of the predators were common. Also during milling, S. marmoratus often moved slightly forward, and S. ulae frequently &quot;danced&quot; in its pelvic fins. At the first moment of the upward rush, these predators dashed forward toward the spawning aggregations from distances varying from 60cm to 3m.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.11369/jji1950.34.76</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;They indicate the inclination of the animal to perform a definite activity.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000839 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000839">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000838"/>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/historical_disposition"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-09-15T10:07:55.482515Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Intention initiator in which incomplete movements only gradually become complete.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">awakening</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:comment>HISTORICAL TERM

This term is part of a historical ethological perspective which is no longer mainstream.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label>awakening initiator</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...the incomplete nesting movements in gulls in early spring as the awakening of reproductive behaviour...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;We wonder if the distinction between awakening movements and incipient movements is quite sharp.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000839"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Intention initiator in which incomplete movements only gradually become complete.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;These are incomplete movements, often released by inadequate stimuli situations, which will gradually become complete when the secretion of the influencing hormones has reached a higher level.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000839"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">awakening</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000839"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>awakening initiator</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000839"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...the incomplete nesting movements in gulls in early spring as the awakening of reproductive behaviour...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000839"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;We wonder if the distinction between awakening movements and incipient movements is quite sharp.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000840 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000840">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000423"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002305"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-09-15T10:16:05.615983Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Exposure that serves a conspicuous structure.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">demonstration movement</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">demonstration exposure</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;A discussion of displays as indicators of quality would be incomplete without comment on the hypothesis that handicaps might evolve as concrete demonstrations to females of the superiority of individual males, the idea being that males able to survive in spite of some burdensome visible character can thereby both prove and signal their superiority over males with lesser handicaps.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Thalassoma cupido...
...a distinctive manner of swimming whereby the pectoral fins are fluttered rapidly, conspicuously displaying their blackened tips.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...we think we have to understand a coordination of muscle contractions that did not exist before. It seems rather unlikely that such a coordination has developed without making use of existing patterns and structures, and that will make it very difficult to decide whether it is really new or not.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000840"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Exposure that serves a conspicuous structure.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;So we will call a demonstration movement a movement that serves a conspicuous structure and that is not in the same form shown in any other part of the behaviour of the animal in question or of related species.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000840"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">demonstration movement</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000840"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">demonstration exposure</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000840"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;A discussion of displays as indicators of quality would be incomplete without comment on the hypothesis that handicaps might evolve as concrete demonstrations to females of the superiority of individual males, the idea being that males able to survive in spite of some burdensome visible character can thereby both prove and signal their superiority over males with lesser handicaps.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1979</dc:date>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000840"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Thalassoma cupido...
...a distinctive manner of swimming whereby the pectoral fins are fluttered rapidly, conspicuously displaying their blackened tips.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstage.jst.go.jp/article/jji1950/24/2/24_2_101/_article/-char/ja/</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000840"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...we think we have to understand a coordination of muscle contractions that did not exist before. It seems rather unlikely that such a coordination has developed without making use of existing patterns and structures, and that will make it very difficult to decide whether it is really new or not.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000841 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000841">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000815"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:nodeID="genid6339"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000026"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-09-16T11:51:46.116147Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Wash routine in which an appendage is laid against the body and, while flapping, turned around.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">flapping and turning</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">flap-and-turn routine</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Restriction rdf:nodeID="genid6339">
        <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
        <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000784"/>
    </owl:Restriction>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000841"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:nodeID="genid6339"/>
        <rdfs:comment>In due course, this might be better given the part of &apos;brushing&apos; (akin to brushing crumbs off ones chest with the fingers) when a relevant term is encountered in the literature and created.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000841"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Wash routine in which an appendage is laid against the body and, while flapping, turned around.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;The pectoral is laid against the body and, while flapping, turned around. This movement might serve to clean the corresponding area of the flank.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000841"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">flapping and turning</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000841"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">flap-and-turn routine</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000842 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000842">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000087"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000003"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
