    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000892 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000892">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000831"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PCO_0001000"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0000383"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-10-20T07:39:00.027785Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Formation in which females perform mating activities with each other.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">homosexual female</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>female-female formation</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Hemichromis bimaculatus...
A female pair performs the same courting activities as a normal one, i.e. jerking, quivering, nipping and skimming. When one of them spawns the other either skims alternately, just like the fertilizing male, or she also lays eggs.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000892"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Formation in which females perform mating activities with each other.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000892"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">homosexual female</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
        <rdfs:comment>Synonym label is constructed from a large section of prose.</rdfs:comment>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000892"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>female-female formation</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000892"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Hemichromis bimaculatus...
A female pair performs the same courting activities as a normal one, i.e. jerking, quivering, nipping and skimming. When one of them spawns the other either skims alternately, just like the fertilizing male, or she also lays eggs.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000893 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000893">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000541"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-10-20T08:29:19.875034Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Switch in which an external cue causes a behaviour change.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>evoking</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">stimulating</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>stimulation</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>synchronisation</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>stimulation switch</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;The majority of sea-anemones present to the eye an almost constant shape which only changes as the result of activities of a very simple kind. This is true, for instance, of Calliactis parasitica... The unstimulated animal rarely departs from the form of a short cylinder surmounted by a disk bearing the tentacles round its edge and the mouth at its centre.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;The stimulating effect of the courting activities does not always seem to shorten the time till spawning. This we observed a few times in pairs of Astronotus ocellatus and C. bimaculatum, of which the male was more energetic than the female. As soon as the female carried out some feeble quivering or nipping-off movements on the substrate, the male rushed on and began to perform the same movements so vigorously that the female was pushed aside and had to leave the stone. Undoubtedly this tended to retard spawning in the female.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;For self-costing aid to be profitable in terms of inclusive fitness it is not enough simply to determine relative fighting ability. The winner must likewise have a relative reproductive capacity superior enough to make helping behavior profitable to the subordinant. In such cases selection would have to define the difference in aggressiveness sufficient to signal a profitable switch to aid-giving behavior, and the cues used (e.g., relative aggressiveness) would have to correlate with reproductive capacity.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000893"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Switch in which an external cue causes a behaviour change.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000893"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>evoking</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1950</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1163/156853981X00220</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
