        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;For self-costing aid to be profitable in terms of inclusive fitness it is not enough simply to determine relative fighting ability. The winner must likewise have a relative reproductive capacity superior enough to make helping behavior profitable to the subordinant. In such cases selection would have to define the difference in aggressiveness sufficient to signal a profitable switch to aid-giving behavior, and the cues used (e.g., relative aggressiveness) would have to correlate with reproductive capacity.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://www.jstor.org/stable/986582</dc:source>
