    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000922 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000922">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000288"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001653"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-11-12T08:31:02.945709Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Relativity that involves an animal positioning themself at a constant angle to another entity.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>alignment</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym xml:lang="en">angling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>alignment relativity</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Nocomis leptocephalus...
Alignment. The female... stationed herself beneath the male with her snout directly below or slightly ahead of his extended pectoral fins. Her body aligned parallel to the pit’s long axis.... She either remained posted beneath the male for a few seconds or immediately initiated the spawning run. During alignment the male tilted the dorsal half of his body to one side as the female’s snout moved beneath his pectoral region. The female’s movement into the pit determined the direction of his sagittal tilt. If she sidled into the pit his dorsal surface tilted towards her entry; if she moved into the pit directly beneath his tail he might angle his body in either direction based on her final position.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000922"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Relativity that involves an animal positioning themself at a constant angle to another entity.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000922"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>alignment</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2000</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000922"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">angling</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2000</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000922"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>alignment relativity</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000922"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Nocomis leptocephalus...
Alignment. The female... stationed herself beneath the male with her snout directly below or slightly ahead of his extended pectoral fins. Her body aligned parallel to the pit’s long axis.... She either remained posted beneath the male for a few seconds or immediately initiated the spawning run. During alignment the male tilted the dorsal half of his body to one side as the female’s snout moved beneath his pectoral region. The female’s movement into the pit determined the direction of his sagittal tilt. If she sidled into the pit his dorsal surface tilted towards her entry; if she moved into the pit directly beneath his tail he might angle his body in either direction based on her final position.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000923 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000923">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000475"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000659"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000057"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ENVO_06105011"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_entity"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-11-12T12:25:18.533324Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Spawning which is in the water column.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>pelagic release</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>pelagic spawning</rdfs:label>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;A temporary spawning ascent into the water column is a nearly universal phenomenon among reef fishes spawning pelagic eggs. This behavioral pattern is usually referred to as the &quot;spawning rush&quot;, although in some species, e.g. ostraciids and pomacanthids, such ascents are extremely slow.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Nocomis leptocephalus...
A successful spawning run always was initiated by the female. With her body pressed to the substrate, she quickly swam about 3—5 cm upstream while quivering her tail and caudal peduncle. The male remained tilted as he promptly accompanied her short run with rapid tail beats. As the female moved forward over the upstream rim of the pit, she gaped and abruptly tossed her head vertically into the water column.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000923"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Spawning which is in the water column.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1007/BF01609520</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;A temporary spawning ascent into the water column is a nearly universal phenomenon among reef fishes spawning pelagic eggs.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000923"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>pelagic release</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1973</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/BF01609520</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...pelagic eggs to be released...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000923"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>pelagic spawning</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1973</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1007/BF01609520</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...spawning pelagic eggs.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000923"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;A temporary spawning ascent into the water column is a nearly universal phenomenon among reef fishes spawning pelagic eggs. This behavioral pattern is usually referred to as the &quot;spawning rush&quot;, although in some species, e.g. ostraciids and pomacanthids, such ascents are extremely slow.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.11369/jji1950.34.76</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000923"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Nocomis leptocephalus...
A successful spawning run always was initiated by the female. With her body pressed to the substrate, she quickly swam about 3—5 cm upstream while quivering her tail and caudal peduncle. The male remained tilted as he promptly accompanied her short run with rapid tail beats. As the female moved forward over the upstream rim of the pit, she gaped and abruptly tossed her head vertically into the water column.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000924 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000924">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000288"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002259"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-11-12T20:50:31.147358Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Relativity that involves close, ongoing interaction.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">accompanying</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">escorting</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">accompanying relativity</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...a small tuberculate male Campostoma anomalum (stoneroller)... accompanied nine separate spawning runs, disrupting six. As the female chub initiated her run with tail quivering, the stoneroller darted towards her and became appressed to her flank opposite the male chub.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...as follower, species do not only interact, but also accompany the nuclear species during a determined period of time...&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000924"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Relativity that involves close, ongoing interaction.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000924"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">accompanying</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2000</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000924"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">escorting</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2013</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1089/zeb.2012.0861</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000924"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
