    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000541"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0001507"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-11-17T17:59:25.882901Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Switch which follows a behaviour disruption.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym xml:lang="en">failure</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>flubbing</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">failure switch</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;...Southeast Asian olive frog, Rana adenopleura...
Attacks by unpaired males were highly successful in disrupting mating by amplectant pairs causing mating failure.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Ardeola ibis...
Head Flick...
Immediately after threatening but failing to dislodge a trespasser.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Nocomis leptocephalus...
Disruption of an interim male or spawning sequence resulted from aggressive interactions with conspecific males, nest associates and egg or fish predators. Disruption of a spawning sequence also resulted if a male or female responded inappropriately to their mate. For example, alignment failed if a female retreated or was displaced from the spawning pit prior to her run. Runs infrequently were disrupted when a female failed to retroflex and exited the pit over the upstream slope.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Another stimulus might stem from the precision of male movements, i.e. from the ‘flubs’, or unsuccessful intromission attempts by males. Such flubs are common in spiders, and if they reflect the male’s competency in copulation, females are predicted to be under selection to use the flub to hit ratio as a criterion in cryptic female choice.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Now let us suppose that the ancient progenitor of our European cuckoo had the habits of the American cuckoo; but that occasionally she laid an egg in another bird’s nest. If the old bird profited by this occasional habit, or if the young were made more vigorous by advantage having been taken of the mistaken maternal instinct of another bird, than by their own mother’s care, encumbered as she can hardly fail to be by having eggs and young of different ages at the same time; then the old birds or the fostered young would gain an advantage.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Switch which follows a behaviour disruption.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">failure</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1987</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.11369/jji1950.34.76</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasRelatedSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>flubbing</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2005</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1017/S1464793104006700</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">failure switch</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;...Southeast Asian olive frog, Rana adenopleura...
Attacks by unpaired males were highly successful in disrupting mating by amplectant pairs causing mating failure.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0083116</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Ardeola ibis...
Head Flick...
Immediately after threatening but failing to dislodge a trespasser.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>https://open.uct.ac.za/handle/11427/13101</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Nocomis leptocephalus...
Disruption of an interim male or spawning sequence resulted from aggressive interactions with conspecific males, nest associates and egg or fish predators. Disruption of a spawning sequence also resulted if a male or female responded inappropriately to their mate. For example, alignment failed if a female retreated or was displaced from the spawning pit prior to her run. Runs infrequently were disrupted when a female failed to retroflex and exited the pit over the upstream slope.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Another stimulus might stem from the precision of male movements, i.e. from the ‘flubs’, or unsuccessful intromission attempts by males. Such flubs are common in spiders, and if they reflect the male’s competency in copulation, females are predicted to be under selection to use the flub to hit ratio as a criterion in cryptic female choice.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1017/S1464793104006700</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000928"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Now let us suppose that the ancient progenitor of our European cuckoo had the habits of the American cuckoo; but that occasionally she laid an egg in another bird’s nest. If the old bird profited by this occasional habit, or if the young were made more vigorous by advantage having been taken of the mistaken maternal instinct of another bird, than by their own mother’s care, encumbered as she can hardly fail to be by having eggs and young of different ages at the same time; then the old birds or the fostered young would gain an advantage.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.5962/bhl.title.82303</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000064"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0000086"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/PATO_0002026"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/disposition_by_quality"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-11-17T18:00:09.38796Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Regime pattern which is transitional, between previous and future stable states.

The future may represent a return or an alternative.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>dynamically unstable transitional regime</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym xml:lang="en">disruption sequence</oboInOwl:hasNarrowSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>dynamically-unstable pattern</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;Cicadas can drive bird predators out of forests both due to the dangerously loud and painful sound, and its disruption to their communication.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;Saimiri sciureus (common squirrel monkey)...
encounters a challenge (a large gap between substrates), disrupting the footfall sequence...&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Disruption of a spawning sequence also resulted if a male or female responded inappropriately to their mate.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Regime pattern which is transitional, between previous and future stable states.

The future may represent a return or an alternative.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy>doi:10.1371/journal.pcbi.1002915</rdfs:isDefinedBy>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;Perturbations, such as disruption induced by predator attacks, or more generally, sources of intrinsic or extrinsic noise, can cause the system to leave its existing dynamically stable state and enter an unstable transitional regime. Depending on the degree and type of perturbation the group may find itself either drawn back towards the previous state, or if perturbed sufficiently far, to be drawn into the alternative dynamically-stable state.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>dynamically unstable transitional regime</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2013</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1371/journal.pcbi.1002915</dc:source>
        <skos:scopeNote>&quot;...dynamically stable state and enter an unstable transitional regime.&quot;</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasNarrowSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">disruption sequence</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">2000</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>dynamically-unstable pattern</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:contributor>https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4277-2151</dc:contributor>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Cicadas can drive bird predators out of forests both due to the dangerously loud and painful sound, and its disruption to their communication.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1111/brv.12891</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#example"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Saimiri sciureus (common squirrel monkey)...
encounters a challenge (a large gap between substrates), disrupting the footfall sequence...&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1002/ajp.23055</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000929"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#scopeNote"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>&quot;Disruption of a spawning sequence also resulted if a male or female responded inappropriately to their mate.&quot;</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


    <!-- http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000930 -->

    <owl:Class rdf:about="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000930">
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000288"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/BFO_0000051"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000385"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <terms:conformsTo rdf:resource="https://github.com/Active-Animals/Ontology/patterns/context_composite"/>
        <oboInOwl:created_by>Ditch Townsend</oboInOwl:created_by>
        <oboInOwl:creation_date>2025-11-18T09:03:25.007859Z</oboInOwl:creation_date>
        <oboInOwl:hasDefinition>Relativity which actively supplants another in its place.</oboInOwl:hasDefinition>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym xml:lang="en">displacing</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>supplanting</oboInOwl:hasExactSynonym>
        <oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>expelling</oboInOwl:hasRelatedSynonym>
        <rdfs:label>displacing relativity</rdfs:label>
        <skos:example>&quot;A. ibis... 
Supplanting runs usually occur when two birds compete for individual food items, with one bird chasing another for one or two metres, and have also been observed in 20 - 30 day nestlings on the ground.&quot;</skos:example>
        <skos:example>&quot;In Nocomis micropogon...
If the female’s snout moved aside or ahead of the male’s snout during their run, the male turned his head and displaced the female from the trough with a lateral head butt.&quot;</skos:example>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000930"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasDefinition"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>Relativity which actively supplants another in its place.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1674/0003-0031(2000)144[0187:SBITBC]2.0.CO;2</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/ACTAN_0000930"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://www.geneontology.org/formats/oboInOwl#hasExactSynonym"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget xml:lang="en">displacing</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <dc:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">1998</dc:date>
        <dc:source>doi:10.1002/(SICI)1098-2337(1998)24:3&lt;197::AID-AB4&gt;3.0.CO;2-I</dc:source>
    </owl:Axiom>
